WEBVTT

01:36.040 --> 01:38.504
There it is. I don't think it was working before.

01:39.704 --> 01:40.191
It's 4.

01:43.312 --> 01:47.010
Okay. This clock's 1 minute fast. Is it? Yeah.

01:47.316 --> 01:50.901
My computer's saying 3:59. I'll give it 1 more second.

01:57.623 --> 02:00.624
Okay.

02:02.189 --> 02:05.854
We're going to go ahead and get started.

02:05.902 --> 02:09.000
This clock up here says 4:00. The one on my wrist says 3:59, but we're

02:09.500 --> 02:12.406
going with it. So we'll welcome you out to the Work session.

02:13.066 --> 02:16.208
It's Tuesday the 7th at 4 o'clock.

02:16.707 --> 02:19.824
We'll go ahead and get started with our first discussion. Item number 1 is

02:20.888 --> 02:24.402
legislative update as part of the contract. David Stewart's here

02:24.934 --> 02:27.061
to give us an update on what he's working on. And then with that,

02:27.876 --> 02:31.728
I think it's been 3 years, is that correct? I think the contract

02:31.825 --> 02:35.306
comes up at the end of this month, is that right? I'd have to

02:35.806 --> 02:39.115
get all the information. Yeah, or is it August? Yeah,

02:39.131 --> 02:42.157
but we'll invite David up to give us an update, and then we can discuss

02:42.692 --> 02:46.467
the contract in the future going forward, and it

02:46.967 --> 02:50.262
will come up later as well. Great, thanks, Mayor, Council.

02:50.555 --> 02:54.309
Great to be here, some new faces. The— so

02:54.809 --> 02:57.781
yeah, we had a great legislative session. Maybe I'll kind of start and work my

02:58.281 --> 03:00.883
way back. In the last little while, we had an election,

03:02.990 --> 03:06.155
and even previous to that, there was an election that more directly impacted the city

03:06.655 --> 03:10.461
as far as for a Republican nominee for State Senate.

03:12.041 --> 03:15.233
A resident, obviously, of Eagle Mountain was able to clear the deck in the first

03:15.249 --> 03:19.014
vote, which was pretty surprising to most, because he had 2 seasoned politicians he was

03:19.030 --> 03:22.524
running against. That says a lot for him and the style and work ethic

03:23.024 --> 03:26.934
that he has, and hopefully bringing that to the Senate. He will be going

03:26.966 --> 03:30.822
against, obviously, Senator Buss in November. I think

03:31.322 --> 03:34.614
most everybody assumes that he, being the Republican nominee, will easily win that seat,

03:34.663 --> 03:38.453
and so then be part of the Senate. Then the

03:38.517 --> 03:42.412
primary, you had, I guess, the nearest as far as proximity,

03:42.524 --> 03:45.489
you had Senator Brammer, who was in a tough fight, but he pulled it out.

03:46.050 --> 03:49.080
You had Senator McKay, who was also in a tough fight. He did not.

03:49.176 --> 03:52.478
He was beat by Doug Fiafia, who currently serves in the House,

03:52.542 --> 03:56.004
so he certainly knows what's going on, but obviously

03:56.504 --> 03:59.001
not Eagle Mountain, but really the south end of Salt Lake County and a piece

03:59.114 --> 04:02.864
of northern Utah County, and a few others. Then one that certainly will

04:03.364 --> 04:07.131
impact could have an impact on the city. President Adams was a

04:07.676 --> 04:11.350
huge supporter of transportation generally across the state and was

04:11.398 --> 04:16.948
a great advocate for us at the city. The nice

04:16.996 --> 04:20.509
thing we have is the likely person to— after

04:21.009 --> 04:25.145
the elections in November, the general elections, then there will be a leadership election

04:25.177 --> 04:28.771
in both the House and the Senate. There's not really anticipated

04:29.271 --> 04:32.795
anybody to challenge the current speaker. There will be one open seat

04:33.295 --> 04:37.014
for that, actually, again, Eagle Mountain, that likely Representative

04:37.514 --> 04:40.889
Griechus will be running for. I would be— she has

04:41.389 --> 04:44.888
a very good chance of winning that seat. She would then be in House leadership.

04:45.816 --> 04:48.918
In the Senate, you'll also have leadership elections. With the Senate President having lost,

04:48.966 --> 04:52.197
that seat's now open. The current Majority Leader, Curt Cullimore,

04:52.245 --> 04:55.868
is planning to run. There's another senator from southern Utah that's

04:56.146 --> 04:59.384
entertaining the idea of maybe running. For us, Senator Cullimore

04:59.884 --> 05:03.265
has been great. He, kind of like Senator Adams, one of his big passions in

05:03.765 --> 05:07.420
life is infrastructure funding, both water and roads and power and energy

05:07.516 --> 05:10.883
and all that. That is one of his focuses

05:10.980 --> 05:14.008
that's been— he chaired the Transportation and IGG,

05:15.023 --> 05:18.485
what they call it, committee, which is the Infrastructure Committee, before he was in leadership,

05:18.630 --> 05:21.463
as President Adams did and as others. And so,

05:21.689 --> 05:25.697
and he's been actually very involved. The mayor and I had the pleasure of meeting

05:25.810 --> 05:28.388
with and Ben as well,

05:29.398 --> 05:32.844
of meeting with the Senate President, also pulled in Senator Cullimore

05:33.344 --> 05:36.178
into that meeting where we talked about all things transportation for the city,

05:37.092 --> 05:40.651
but specifically, and I'm never going to remember this, it's not

05:40.763 --> 05:44.594
Mid-Valley, it's Lake Mountain? Okay. Still better than

05:44.931 --> 05:48.041
the other name. Brown Ridge?

05:48.874 --> 05:51.760
Crown Ridge, okay. So we talked about that,

05:52.705 --> 05:56.296
and that now will take us kind of into the session. So Senator Cullimore was

05:56.328 --> 05:59.384
there, he's up to speed, he was one that was integral in helping get the

05:59.884 --> 06:03.213
corridor preservation dollars that we had to preserve the Cedar Valley Expressway.

06:03.326 --> 06:06.863
He also worked closely with us on—

06:07.056 --> 06:11.832
and Representative Grecious, who was huge on getting— and probably

06:12.332 --> 06:14.849
it's hard to really explain how difficult it is, because to most people it's like,

06:14.865 --> 06:17.669
oh, we just put it in a bill, it's no big deal. But in transferring

06:17.766 --> 06:20.729
the jurisdiction of the— Lake Mountain Road.

06:22.533 --> 06:26.730
Lake Mountain Road to the state, that was slated to be a local

06:26.764 --> 06:30.163
road. The reality is the cost of that would prohibit us from probably ever

06:30.663 --> 06:33.868
building it. There's just not going to be enough money from us or from MAG,

06:34.353 --> 06:36.051
or there's just not enough discretionary dollars.

06:38.193 --> 06:42.462
There's a process that you generally go through in moving roads to the state system,

06:42.526 --> 06:45.820
and usually it has to be an existing road. We were able, again,

06:45.916 --> 06:49.372
in working with Representative Griecious and working with Senator Cullimore and

06:49.872 --> 06:53.470
others, to— in a bill of jurisdictional transfers

06:53.970 --> 06:56.807
that they do every year, and they do a couple, and usually it's technical type

06:57.307 --> 07:00.497
stuff. Right. But we're able to put this road that's not yet built, just on

07:00.529 --> 07:03.999
a plan, and put it into the state system. So it's now

07:04.240 --> 07:06.907
going to be funded and ultimately built by the state.

07:07.518 --> 07:10.072
That was the first, as we kind of talked leading into the session, that was

07:10.184 --> 07:13.462
the critical thing that had to happen. It was something that took a lot of

07:13.962 --> 07:16.514
effort leading up to and kind of getting UDOT on board and everybody on board

07:17.014 --> 07:19.374
so that it wasn't a fight, and it ended up just sailing through.

07:19.888 --> 07:23.198
And what that does is that was the first domino. Now what that sets us

07:23.698 --> 07:27.226
up for is MAG had also kind of helped prep the work for

07:27.274 --> 07:31.124
the work we did this session, had done a study funded at MAG

07:31.526 --> 07:35.200
that kind of looks at— looked at different potential alignments and things

07:35.232 --> 07:38.168
and how it would work. Now that it's a state road, the state will have

07:38.184 --> 07:42.499
to do their own process. They're looking overall at the Redwood Extension

07:43.157 --> 07:46.655
and SR 73 and all kinds of— as a whole

07:46.783 --> 07:49.879
region, if you will. And this is now included in that. Thank you.

07:49.992 --> 07:53.855
Analysis and study. So that was the next step. Once that step is completed,

07:53.903 --> 07:56.933
and I would guess it will be anywhere from 1 to 2 years, once that

07:56.965 --> 08:00.412
step is completed, you're then— once they kind of get a rough alignment that

08:00.912 --> 08:04.547
they determine, okay, this is where we want to now have an EIS actually

08:05.044 --> 08:08.619
formally study and evaluate and go through all the potential

08:08.667 --> 08:12.194
pitfalls and problems and see if it works. Once you

08:12.694 --> 08:16.266
get to the point of the EIS, you can then start preserving corridor And

08:16.282 --> 08:19.615
then you go through that EIS. That process will then take a couple years.

08:20.127 --> 08:22.835
And then at that point, at the conclusion of that, that's when you're ready to

08:23.335 --> 08:28.059
actually fund and build the road. It takes the

08:28.559 --> 08:31.087
analysis and the study will give you kind of a rough scope and cost.

08:31.183 --> 08:34.869
The EIS will then fine-tune what that is. And then we'll know exactly how

08:35.369 --> 08:37.577
much to fund. And so again, just kind of tell you that to kind of

08:37.593 --> 08:40.637
prepare you. This is— and this will be something that, you know, it's a couple

08:41.137 --> 08:44.886
years here, a couple years there. So, you know, optimistically— 10 years? potentially could be

08:44.950 --> 08:48.818
building in the next 5 to 6 years, which going from just

08:48.882 --> 08:52.189
a colored, shaded, rough line on a map

08:52.689 --> 08:56.121
that didn't mean anything to now actually having the kind of

08:56.621 --> 09:00.615
the stepped process been kicked off is I think a huge testament

09:01.115 --> 09:04.275
to your guys' focus and kind of laser focus on that this last couple years

09:04.307 --> 09:08.271
to get that moving. Because until we started having those dominoes fall,

09:08.785 --> 09:11.898
it just kept— keeps getting pushed out further. And the nice part is,

09:12.220 --> 09:15.994
From a funding perspective, is that puts us into a realm that there is potential

09:16.026 --> 09:19.078
available funding. Whereas if you know we want to show up tomorrow and try and

09:19.578 --> 09:22.130
build a two hundred you know hundred to two hundred million dollar road, there's not

09:22.630 --> 09:26.563
that free money. It's already been committed. So it's now in the queue, and what

09:27.063 --> 09:32.169
we'll be working closely with you know likely potentially Senator

09:32.669 --> 09:35.815
Brooks and Representative Gresham among others will be to

09:35.847 --> 09:37.774
kind of keep that. It's that fine line of.

09:39.190 --> 09:42.155
Councilman Brett Wright and I were talking about being the squeaky wheel that's just enough

09:42.655 --> 09:45.784
that people pay attention and keep doing it, but not so bad that they're annoyed

09:46.284 --> 09:48.749
and kind of kick you off to the side. And so it'll be that weaving

09:48.782 --> 09:51.973
that, but continuing that process so that we don't delay it any further.

09:52.021 --> 09:54.872
But just to prepare you, it is going to— there are steps and it will

09:54.921 --> 09:58.416
take time to do that. And so that was really the

09:58.480 --> 10:00.594
main focus of the session, was getting through that.

10:02.433 --> 10:06.125
And then worked on— there were some other things dealing with some legal

10:06.286 --> 10:10.139
issues that affected cities where there was what they call the Gardner decision

10:10.639 --> 10:14.572
in the Utah Supreme Court. What that essentially said is currently in litigation and

10:14.588 --> 10:17.673
stuff, you can't— it's called billed versus paid.

10:17.930 --> 10:21.321
Historically, the way it worked is a jury is not allowed

10:21.821 --> 10:25.021
to hear what was actually paid. So if you came in and let's say

10:25.521 --> 10:29.119
your medical bills and everything else were $250,000, but you were able

10:29.135 --> 10:32.297
to settle with the hospital, the doctors, and everything else, and it only cost $100,000,

10:33.012 --> 10:36.442
you can't ever tell the jury that it was— you're only out of pocket $100,000.

10:36.763 --> 10:39.936
They only know what the build was of $250,000. So that

10:40.049 --> 10:43.623
artificially, because the punitives and everything else are calculated off that,

10:43.671 --> 10:47.406
can astronomically escalate the cost. And what that means

10:47.438 --> 10:50.323
to you guys as self-insured, just a lot more cost to the city. So the

10:50.823 --> 10:54.651
League came out hugely opposed, worked with the mayor to meet with our

10:55.151 --> 10:58.626
delegation, as well as worked with everybody else. We were able to— so the bill,

10:58.818 --> 11:02.203
what it did, so the Supreme Court turned the billed versus paid on its head.

11:02.251 --> 11:06.282
So it said, no, you can introduce what was paid. They get to see

11:06.782 --> 11:09.734
that and then make a determination. There was a bill run to kind of take

11:10.234 --> 11:13.668
it back to what the previous had been of, no, you can't show

11:13.732 --> 11:16.686
what was actually paid. And we were able to defeat that bill. So that was

11:17.441 --> 11:21.198
surprisingly in Utah actually a tough fight. There's still a lot of attorneys that

11:21.698 --> 11:24.361
actually are in the legislature and care a lot about that bill.

11:25.646 --> 11:28.921
So you had that and, you know, then there's continued discussions about water.

11:29.962 --> 11:33.707
and meeting with UDOT on transportation and not just Lake

11:34.207 --> 11:37.160
Mountain, right? Lake and the river, which is the mountain.

11:38.646 --> 11:40.680
Okay, that works. That's okay, perfect.

11:42.182 --> 11:45.434
But yeah, so I don't know, that's kind of rambled on there,

11:45.547 --> 11:49.105
but that was really, I think, kind of a quick snapshot. Happy to, at this

11:49.605 --> 11:52.008
point, if you had any other questions or things, but happy to talk about that.

11:55.520 --> 11:59.304
If there's— well, a couple of things, I guess. First of all, the highlight that

11:59.804 --> 12:02.732
he brushed over is over June and July, we really put a lot of pressure

12:03.232 --> 12:07.060
on both the House and the Senate, and of course got that meeting

12:07.076 --> 12:11.610
with Kohlmore and Stuart Adams to talk

12:12.110 --> 12:16.084
about our needs here. And so that was a big step, and most

12:16.584 --> 12:19.378
of those meetings were all arranged with Dave and with Evan. Yeah.

12:20.302 --> 12:24.028
working together to get us all in the same room. So huge success there.

12:24.863 --> 12:28.460
Uh, is there any other questions on what was covered? Because if not,

12:28.476 --> 12:31.110
I want to move. I'm going to jump around a little bit just for his

12:31.142 --> 12:34.595
sake and time. Sorry, Mayor, real quick, let me— and I, and I apologize,

12:34.611 --> 12:37.630
you mentioned it, but I want to make sure— Evan's been a huge asset to

12:38.130 --> 12:40.762
the city, great to work with as far as being a communicator, kind of in

12:41.262 --> 12:44.712
between the different departments, yourselves, and everything else. But he and I worked great through

12:45.212 --> 12:48.886
the session, coordinated, you know, certainly maybe not quite every day, but several times

12:49.386 --> 12:52.847
a week on key issues, on meetings and where things were. And I apologize for

12:53.347 --> 12:56.343
not bringing that up. But no, Evan was certainly, you know, was a huge asset

12:56.843 --> 13:00.096
up there and attended all the LPC and the league meetings and has built

13:00.596 --> 13:04.009
good relationships there. So a huge, huge asset to me representing

13:04.025 --> 13:07.345
you, but certainly to you as a city. So. Yeah, the entire team up there

13:07.425 --> 13:10.632
this year was awesome. And still we're continuing to do that stuff.

13:11.691 --> 13:15.684
So. Just one question for you. I really appreciate the update.

13:16.293 --> 13:20.014
It's so helpful. We need the money, we need the roads built,

13:20.399 --> 13:24.392
and we appreciate all your efforts in helping us do that. There was one piece

13:24.892 --> 13:29.957
of legislation that was kind of advancing that talked about data center revenue not

13:30.021 --> 13:33.950
going to the city but being used more— Statewide.

13:34.094 --> 13:37.799
Globally or statewide. And that was super concerning. I mean,

13:37.943 --> 13:41.998
we have a lot invested as a city and time

13:42.046 --> 13:45.843
invested. And just curious, do you think that is a reality that that

13:45.891 --> 13:49.640
could pass or just would love your thoughts

13:50.140 --> 13:53.421
on that? And if there's, I just hate hearing any of that. It's like— Yeah,

13:54.046 --> 13:57.139
no, it certainly, and you know, and obviously data centers were kind of the,

13:57.972 --> 14:01.416
it wasn't necessarily, or the idea wasn't necessarily fixated,

14:01.432 --> 14:04.621
but obviously that would affect Eagle Mountain greatly. But it was kind of this

14:05.121 --> 14:08.820
idea of you have these huge infrastructure cap investment type projects that are

14:09.320 --> 14:12.942
in certain areas of the state, and should there be a portion

14:12.958 --> 14:17.434
of that revenue that then is shared statewide to help lower

14:17.450 --> 14:20.402
income tax, or to help do other types of statewide purposes?

14:20.980 --> 14:24.252
So, you certainly, there are, to answer your question, there are certainly advocates for that,

14:24.766 --> 14:28.343
but there's equally, and more, if you will, of saying,

14:28.375 --> 14:32.305
no, there are impacts to the local communities. Now, if there's a new tax,

14:32.370 --> 14:35.273
or something else that you want to try to put, or to, you know,

14:35.353 --> 14:38.065
capitalize on those types of investments and spread that,

14:38.321 --> 14:41.674
that might be something. But to try and come into a community,

14:41.995 --> 14:45.267
that argument's currently winning the day. With all the news

14:45.316 --> 14:48.845
media around data centers and everything else, they're not quite as exciting, at least at

14:49.345 --> 14:52.663
the moment publicly. I think this— I think it'll all kind of tamp down and

14:53.163 --> 14:56.224
go away, honestly. But I guess a long way to say it's still there.

14:56.384 --> 14:59.898
It's still a threat. Um-hum. I don't think the current makeup

15:00.058 --> 15:03.621
now that there's anybody that's a key member or members of

15:03.653 --> 15:06.062
leadership that are like, you know, pounding, this is what we got to do.

15:06.335 --> 15:10.140
So far, it's— there's enough and it's broad enough beyond

15:10.156 --> 15:14.748
just data centers that, you know, it really punishes your rural areas, your development,

15:15.213 --> 15:18.810
you know, areas that have development, you know, your built-out areas.

15:18.906 --> 15:21.395
They're like, oh yeah, this is a great idea, we should do that. We got

15:21.895 --> 15:25.633
all ours, now let's, you know, tax and bring in everybody else's. But because of

15:26.147 --> 15:29.567
the— if it were just fixated on data centers, that would make me more scared,

15:29.599 --> 15:32.565
honestly, because that's unique enough and doesn't affect enough communities.

15:33.029 --> 15:35.641
But as a lot of times in most legislation, they kept it just broad enough

15:35.769 --> 15:38.830
that it created a lot of allies to kind of— and so as long as

15:39.330 --> 15:41.746
it's that, I think we maintain. But that's something we'll certainly need to be watching

15:41.778 --> 15:45.063
for, because I don't see it going away. I mean, they're always looking for other

15:45.563 --> 15:48.988
ways to bring more revenue in for state kind of projects

15:49.488 --> 15:52.513
and stuff. Because outside of income tax, I mean, you have so much, you know,

15:52.577 --> 15:56.871
your gas tax goes towards roads, sales, you know, income tax funds education,

15:56.984 --> 16:00.366
and you got a sales tax that basically funds everything else, and a big chunk

16:00.866 --> 16:03.445
of that's dedicated to transportation and other things. So there's only a small piece,

16:03.558 --> 16:07.471
honestly, that really funds most everything in the state government beyond

16:07.487 --> 16:11.352
transportation and Health and Human Services and stuff. So it's

16:11.852 --> 16:15.153
something we'll need to watch for. Thank you. I think, if I remember right,

16:15.281 --> 16:17.943
a lot— when that verbiage first came out, we did a lot of work.

16:18.072 --> 16:22.113
Evan did some private meetings. I'm trying to remember if it was 507

16:22.613 --> 16:26.108
or whichever one it was, but it was We got some of that

16:26.157 --> 16:29.919
language adjusted anyway as it's coming forward. There's still room to massage that.

16:31.570 --> 16:34.319
But definitely between the team, we were on that.

16:35.743 --> 16:39.042
So this will also tie in, but I wanted to jump to

16:39.542 --> 16:43.051
item 15A on our consent agenda that has to do with

16:43.551 --> 16:47.639
the agreement and renewal with Executive Consulting,

16:48.379 --> 16:52.240
just so that he can Use his time wisely and we can finish this item

16:52.740 --> 16:55.750
since they're related. So I know there's a question here.

16:55.820 --> 16:59.160
Go ahead. I'd like your name on it.

16:59.460 --> 17:01.360
If we're going to pay for it, I want to know you're the one up

17:01.860 --> 17:04.860
there doing it personally. Oh, on the contract? Yeah. Oh, sure. I mean— I don't

17:05.360 --> 17:07.460
want the— I don't want your services to be able to be sold to somebody

17:07.960 --> 17:11.360
else and we're paying for Dave. Yeah. No, and I— and honestly, a lot of

17:11.860 --> 17:14.580
my contracts, I mean, for tax purposes and other stuff, it'll list the— and for

17:15.080 --> 17:18.081
legal, it lists it. But usually, and I'm sure your attorney can do this,

17:18.162 --> 17:21.864
and if not, I've got— I know other clients where it specifically says services

17:21.880 --> 17:25.594
to be provided by X, and essentially if whatever that changes,

17:25.626 --> 17:29.463
then that, you know, essentially at the client discretion voids the contract type

17:29.963 --> 17:33.229
thing. So yeah, that's— Yeah, I'm totally fine doing the extension with that

17:33.342 --> 17:35.872
caveat. Perfect, yeah, happy to do it. 2 years, I think, is that right?

17:36.034 --> 17:38.272
I think it's 3. Is it 3 years? Yeah.

17:41.682 --> 17:44.856
Any other questions on that? So the one question I had was,

17:45.625 --> 17:49.472
so the contract stipulated 2 meetings, it didn't stipulate

17:49.972 --> 17:52.565
a tempo. So we have a 2-year contract from what I was reading, we can

17:53.065 --> 17:56.396
go back and look at it. Are we comfortable with 2 meetings, which would mean

17:56.428 --> 17:59.826
basically a meeting a year, or does the council feel like we should have more

17:59.874 --> 18:02.615
than that? 'Cause I'm wondering if we need 2 meetings a year,

18:03.785 --> 18:06.590
not necessarily in person, it could be remote, but it seems to me— Yeah,

18:06.606 --> 18:09.539
no, and what we've tended to do is a lot of times,

18:10.534 --> 18:13.504
kind of early on in the session because it is hard to be here in

18:14.004 --> 18:17.773
person during the session remotely. And then previous years when you've had kind

18:18.273 --> 18:20.919
of a retreat, I think in like the fall-ish, if I recall, or summer,

18:21.352 --> 18:24.948
have Zoomed into that before. And so it's kind of— to me, I see some

18:24.980 --> 18:27.933
of those as— I mean, if there needs to be more, certainly can, or whatever

