36:11.605 --> 36:14.849
The code just says 3 members want to bring it back. So the

36:15.349 --> 36:18.606
issue is that during the meeting, if you want to reconsider an

36:18.638 --> 36:22.443
item that's already been done, then it has to be 2 members who voted

36:22.943 --> 36:26.372
in the majority. want to bring it back in the same meeting. After the meeting,

36:26.581 --> 36:29.698
it can be any 3 council members. Just 3 council members? Yeah. Okay.

36:30.421 --> 36:34.774
Thank you. So could it— does the mayor have any option to put

36:35.274 --> 36:39.259
it on an agenda? So where it's an item for

36:39.759 --> 36:42.392
reconsideration, it would be 3 council members? 3 council.

36:43.420 --> 36:46.152
So look, let me just say that I would support that action. I mean,

36:46.168 --> 36:49.134
I'll just say that I would support that. What I would ask is that somebody

36:49.150 --> 36:51.808
on the staff write that and I'll sign it. and see if there's 2 other

36:52.308 --> 36:55.321
council members that are willing to sign it with. Agreed. Agreed.

36:57.062 --> 36:59.318
Sorry, I would like to see it come back as well. There you go,

36:59.527 --> 37:03.410
you got 3. Thank you, that's what I needed. Bring it back

37:03.910 --> 37:07.728
next time. Okay, so Lacey will draft a letter, the council can

37:08.584 --> 37:11.816
sign that, and we'll see it on an upcoming agenda.

37:13.310 --> 37:18.064
There— is there any questions for the applicants? Anything I

37:18.564 --> 37:20.501
don't think so. I think we're good. All right,

37:22.500 --> 37:25.917
moving on to item 1D, off-street parking.

37:28.167 --> 37:31.614
The off-street trails master plan is Mr.

37:31.743 --> 37:32.472
Steve Mumford.

37:35.720 --> 37:38.960
Thank you, Mayor and Council. Um,

37:38.993 --> 37:42.070
excited to present the off-street trails master plan to you today.

37:43.668 --> 37:47.540
This is a project that was funded by a

37:48.040 --> 37:51.008
UDOT Technical Planning Assistance Grant several years ago.

37:51.589 --> 37:55.504
Took a bit to get started on it, but we

37:56.004 --> 37:59.771
finally got started a while back. Todd Black was our project manager

38:00.271 --> 38:04.166
for the project. There was a time there after his passing where we

38:04.360 --> 38:08.175
had to transition, and I volunteered to

38:08.593 --> 38:12.559
to manage the project after that. We had on

38:13.059 --> 38:16.645
our committee, we had me, Brad Hickman,

38:16.774 --> 38:19.962
Zach Hilton, and Andrew Knowlton. And then

38:20.622 --> 38:24.311
as part of that project, we hired Bike Utah as

38:24.328 --> 38:27.695
our consultant for the plan. And Cameron Carter is here from Bike

38:27.711 --> 38:32.014
Utah to present that to you. We also reviewed

38:32.514 --> 38:36.364
the plan with our Community Services Board. At their last

38:36.864 --> 38:40.141
meeting a couple weeks ago, and they had

38:40.641 --> 38:44.707
a few suggestions for some minor changes to that, and mostly

38:44.756 --> 38:47.011
were excited to see this project moving forward.

38:48.480 --> 38:52.499
Just wanted to note that I

38:52.531 --> 38:56.598
am, as part of the implementation, we've already begun implementation of

38:56.614 --> 38:59.869
the plan, and I've started

39:00.369 --> 39:04.324
working with Sitla and other landowners up in the Hidden Valley area

39:04.824 --> 39:08.504
to obtain easements and legal access to many of the trails

39:08.617 --> 39:09.909
that exist up in that area.

39:12.482 --> 39:15.804
And Sitla has come out, met with the

39:16.304 --> 39:20.332
Lake Mountain Trails Association and the city, did a tour of the

39:20.832 --> 39:23.975
property up there, and seems

39:24.475 --> 39:27.958
to be moving towards approving those easements and working

39:27.990 --> 39:31.225
with us on that. So excited about that and

39:31.725 --> 39:35.124
the project moving forward. With that, any questions before

39:35.174 --> 39:36.380
we have Cameron get started?

39:38.206 --> 39:39.677
Okay. Go ahead, Cameron. Thanks.

39:45.353 --> 39:49.331
I am Cameron Carter. I am the planner and project

39:49.831 --> 39:53.510
manager with Bike Utah. I'm here representing Bike Utah's planning team as well as Shapeshift

39:54.010 --> 39:57.665
Terrain Parks. We were the consultants for this project. I'm going to

39:58.324 --> 40:02.183
speak briefly about the plan. High-level objectives,

40:02.667 --> 40:06.254
stakeholder engagement we did for the project, and then just an outline

40:06.512 --> 40:09.719
of the— how the plan is written. I'll then

40:09.767 --> 40:13.700
talk about the 4 main chapters. This plan is written

40:13.911 --> 40:16.986
so that each chapter is meant to be a standalone chapter with

40:17.486 --> 40:21.055
its own recommendations. So we'll get into that, and then we will close

40:21.120 --> 40:22.514
with immediate next steps.

40:24.410 --> 40:26.848
So some of the main objectives.

40:28.139 --> 40:31.848
Starting off, organize the city's trail data into an actionable database.

40:32.121 --> 40:34.996
When we started this project, we were given some data from the city.

40:35.991 --> 40:39.588
This was not a complete dataset of all trails, so we combined other

40:40.088 --> 40:43.666
sources from, from the state and private sources. We also added

40:43.955 --> 40:47.953
several other datasets, including land ownership

40:48.339 --> 40:52.112
and some other data into a more actionable database that you can use

40:52.612 --> 40:56.258
for future planning. Secondly, it was identifying low priority

40:56.758 --> 41:00.419
trails for realignment or removal, and identifying high priority trails for preservation.

41:00.960 --> 41:04.896
There are a lot of trails in Eagle Mountain, and so one of the considerations

41:05.089 --> 41:08.576
throughout this whole process was narrowing down

41:08.688 --> 41:12.591
the list into a set of high priority trails that the city can more effectively

41:12.624 --> 41:16.356
manage. Another objective

41:16.421 --> 41:20.210
was redesigning the Mountain Ranch Bike Park. There are several challenges that we'll

41:20.258 --> 41:23.567
get into that needed to be addressed in that bike park.

41:24.178 --> 41:28.018
And then assessing long-term viability

41:28.130 --> 41:31.716
of the NICA course and establishing wayfinding and signage guidance. So we'll get into

41:31.748 --> 41:34.841
all of those. And then also,

41:35.968 --> 41:39.415
just what we're talking about here is off-street recreational trails.

41:39.528 --> 41:44.000
These are mostly soft surface trails. And then we're also looking

41:44.500 --> 41:47.883
at supporting infrastructure for the trail system, meaning trailheads, signage,

41:47.900 --> 41:51.591
parking areas, and restrooms. I also want to note that

41:52.076 --> 41:55.792
although most trail users are mountain bikes, we also considered

41:56.019 --> 41:59.499
other users and all trails, ATV trails,

41:59.531 --> 42:02.835
equestrian trails. So this was a comprehensive look at

42:02.900 --> 42:03.856
all trails within the city.

42:06.648 --> 42:10.541
I mentioned we engaged with many stakeholders to get a sense

42:11.041 --> 42:14.386
of what are the opportunities and challenges with the current

42:14.418 --> 42:18.237
system. So some of those opportunities: lower elevation,

42:18.606 --> 42:21.867
more easily accessible compared to many of the Wasatch Front trails,

42:22.703 --> 42:25.711
especially early and late season. Because of that lower elevation,

42:26.146 --> 42:29.517
people are able to access the trail system earlier in the spring and later into

42:29.953 --> 42:33.727
the winter. There's a greater variety of terrain and skill

42:34.227 --> 42:37.733
level options. Deadwood and Prayer Flag

42:37.928 --> 42:41.535
trails were consistently ranked as priority trails for many

42:41.567 --> 42:45.610
users. And then users also like some of the existing trailheads,

42:45.674 --> 42:49.577
especially the Hidden Hollow Trailhead. Some of the challenges:

42:50.251 --> 42:54.507
there's very limited and inconsistent signage across

42:55.007 --> 42:58.972
the whole system. There's many conflict points where other trails are crossing

42:59.405 --> 43:03.099
each other without proper signage, creating safety issues. There's a lot

43:03.599 --> 43:06.841
of erosion and damaged features, especially in the bike park. There's many

43:06.953 --> 43:09.893
wooden features that are in disrepair, which creates safety issues.

43:11.999 --> 43:15.553
Trails are frequently disrupted by development, especially in lower

43:16.053 --> 43:19.965
Hidden Valley and upper Hidden Valley. And then it gets very hot

43:20.465 --> 43:23.418
in the summer, so there's a need for additional water access and restrooms.

43:25.919 --> 43:29.372
So as I mentioned, there are 4 main chapters to this plan. First is the

43:29.437 --> 43:33.680
trail network assessment, which essentially establishes this trail database,

43:34.519 --> 43:37.356
identifies high and low priority trails, and values trail conditions.

43:38.046 --> 43:41.459
Chapter 3 is about the Mountain Ranch Bike Park. Chapter 4 is

43:41.959 --> 43:45.754
about the NICA course, identifying development conflicts and then options for

43:46.254 --> 43:49.567
future NICA course development. And then Chapter 5, trailheads and trail signage.

43:50.240 --> 43:54.278
So we'll now get into each of those very briefly. Starting with

43:54.391 --> 43:58.108
Chapter 2, Trail Network Assessment. So we

43:58.413 --> 44:02.018
divided the network into 5 regions. There's the Mountain Ranch Bike

44:02.518 --> 44:05.873
Park, Upper and Lower Hidden Valley, The Southern Trail section,

44:05.989 --> 44:06.747
and then Firefly.

44:09.227 --> 44:12.779
This slide shows an example of what this database looks like. So,

44:14.129 --> 44:17.875
you know, for each region we're showing the trail name, the type of trail,

44:18.246 --> 44:21.634
length, users. We brought in usership data from Strava,

44:21.650 --> 44:25.312
so it's a measure of how many annual users

44:25.506 --> 44:28.865
are reporting their trips on these trails, and then

44:29.609 --> 44:31.048
difficulty and Trailforks rating.

44:34.134 --> 44:37.949
In addition to that, we also did these assessments. So we're looking at

44:38.449 --> 44:41.375
landowner conflicts, development conflicts, and environmental conflicts.

44:42.712 --> 44:45.665
These are mainly looking at who owns the land that the trails are on.

44:46.327 --> 44:49.618
How does this— how, you know, are there any

44:49.715 --> 44:53.511
overlapping areas with future development? And then are there any intersecting

44:54.035 --> 44:56.298
wetland or stream features for these trails?

44:58.429 --> 45:01.700
As far as land ownership conflicts go, this map shows

45:01.893 --> 45:04.186
the predominant landowner for each trail.

45:05.405 --> 45:08.676
Overall, land ownership is highly fragmented across Eagle Mountain.

45:08.949 --> 45:12.011
44% of trail miles are on Sitla land,

45:12.396 --> 45:16.550
24% are on Eagle Mountain city-owned property, 21% private,

45:16.566 --> 45:20.302
and 11% BLM land.

45:21.761 --> 45:25.822
Recommendations for land ownership. We recommend prioritizing the

45:25.950 --> 45:29.708
Eagle Mountain city-owned trails and formalizing trails on Sitla

45:30.208 --> 45:33.626
BLM and private property. So one thing to note within city boundaries,

45:33.738 --> 45:37.287
only 12% of the trails that are on Sitla land have an active

45:37.787 --> 45:41.205
agreement. Most of them do not. So it makes it very difficult for the

45:41.705 --> 45:44.947
city to manage those trails and illegal for people to access them.

45:45.862 --> 45:49.443
And then if agreements are not possible, we would recommend rerouting

45:49.492 --> 45:54.019
or even closing those trails. Development conflicts.

45:54.198 --> 45:57.898
We we looked at two main developments.

45:57.996 --> 46:02.954
First is the Mid Valley Road, or sorry.

46:03.067 --> 46:06.595
First, we'll look just at the development conflicts. This shows the lower Hidden Valley

46:06.627 --> 46:08.463
on the left and upper Hidden Valley on the right.

46:10.227 --> 46:13.748
The yellow areas on this map are developed areas,

46:13.845 --> 46:17.527
essentially neighborhoods that will be developed, and then that orange line is

46:17.640 --> 46:21.416
the Mid Valley Road, which I know has a different name now. But you

46:21.916 --> 46:25.430
can see it crosses through, through this area that has many

46:25.527 --> 46:29.307
existing trails in it. This map

46:29.807 --> 46:33.430
shows essentially the opposite. We've highlighted all the trails that can be preserved,

46:33.608 --> 46:37.521
so the ones that do not intersect with development or Mid Valley Road are

46:37.553 --> 46:41.164
shown in green, yellow, or red. The ones that

46:41.245 --> 46:44.868
are conflicting are those gray dotted lines. So there are a

46:45.368 --> 46:48.687
lot of conflicts, a lot of trails that will be developed Essentially removed

46:49.187 --> 46:52.793
from the network. One thing to note here is that within

46:53.293 --> 46:56.882
the master development agreement, there is this trail corridor in the middle. We think

46:57.382 --> 47:00.635
that is a really great opportunity to preserve a lot of trails through the neighborhood.

47:04.009 --> 47:07.678
This this shows those conflicts. We've listed out all of the

47:08.178 --> 47:11.395
trails that are either significantly impacted, meaning over

47:11.895 --> 47:15.484
90% of that trail will be developed. Or majority

47:15.517 --> 47:19.754
impacted between 50 and 90%. So again,

47:19.867 --> 47:23.554
many trails are going to be developed, and so there is a need to consider

47:23.847 --> 47:27.230
what to do with these trails before development actually occurs.

47:30.329 --> 47:33.962
Recommendations: we recommend prioritizing high priority trails,

47:35.367 --> 47:37.595
preserving city-owned trails within lower Hidden Valley,

47:38.906 --> 47:42.823
and then planning connections. within the Hidden Hollow Trail corridor. I mentioned

47:43.323 --> 47:47.311
that, that middle section, and then planning crossings as Mid Valley Road

47:47.811 --> 47:47.955
is developed.

47:50.633 --> 47:54.496
Environmental conflicts. So this map shows the mainly just

47:54.996 --> 47:57.845
streams that are occurring within Eagle Mountain City.

47:59.140 --> 48:02.389
Trails that cross streams are more likely to be damaged by erosion,

48:02.731 --> 48:06.127
and so it is best practice to avoid those

48:06.877 --> 48:10.069
those areas. And when it's— there's also design features

48:10.165 --> 48:14.047
that you can add to prevent erosion. As far

48:14.547 --> 48:17.977
as recommendations go, we've identified which trails have those conflicts.

48:18.458 --> 48:22.404
If there are few conflicts, those can be improved through design,

48:22.532 --> 48:24.666
improved drainage, or rerouting a short section.

48:25.404 --> 48:29.398
Trails that have significant environmental conflicts, we recommend closing them entirely.

48:29.991 --> 48:33.729
And then we also recommend using environmental screening for future trail planning

48:33.970 --> 48:37.708
Just to make sure your investments are effective and not going

48:38.208 --> 48:39.448
to be damaged by future erosion.

48:41.866 --> 48:45.661
And then lastly, for the trail database, we also did a field assessment

48:46.161 --> 48:50.200
where we actually went and rode all of these trails and identified issues related to

48:50.700 --> 48:53.872
trail definition, meaning it's not clear where the trail is.

48:54.532 --> 48:57.898
Also erosion, lack of signage, safety hazards,

48:58.397 --> 49:02.313
unsanctioned trails, and trail width issues. So again,

49:02.362 --> 49:05.742
this is all part of that trail database that we have provided for the city

49:05.839 --> 49:09.793
to use as you're addressing these issues and planning future

49:10.293 --> 49:13.919
trails. Next chapter,

49:14.000 --> 49:17.689
Mountain Ranch Bike Park. This one is quite a bit more actionable.

49:19.758 --> 49:23.207
This shows the current layout for the Mountain Ranch

49:23.239 --> 49:27.208
Bike Park, and the yellow box in that bottom

49:27.708 --> 49:30.948
left, that is the trailhead on Eagle Mountain Road. There are

49:31.237 --> 49:34.896
quite a few issues with this layout. For one, there's a lot of overlapping

49:34.928 --> 49:38.908
and informal trails that create a sort of spider webbing that

49:39.004 --> 49:42.455
has a lot of safety issues. It's also difficult for users to navigate.

49:43.193 --> 49:46.290
There's mismatch between posted and actual difficulty

49:46.338 --> 49:50.286
for these trails. Many of them are primarily for advanced

49:50.335 --> 49:53.902
riders so that there's not enough Features for

49:54.175 --> 49:57.702
beginners, limited access to upper elevations.

49:57.815 --> 50:01.164
There's no dedicated climbing trail, poor wayfinding, and then

50:01.438 --> 50:04.423
damaged trail features, meaning those wooden features that are in disrepair.

50:07.178 --> 50:10.347
This shows the redesigned concept. So you can see

50:10.847 --> 50:14.821
there is a dedicated uphill access trail in light green that

50:15.178 --> 50:18.568
goes all the way up and connects to each of those other trails.

50:19.151 --> 50:21.782
Some improved trail organization, flow, connectivity,

50:22.951 --> 50:26.979
clear difficult progression. So there's actual green trails

50:27.340 --> 50:29.883
for beginners, then blue and black diamond.

50:31.473 --> 50:34.208
Improved wayfinding. There's a new skills area for beginners,

50:35.596 --> 50:38.817
an improved jump line that would be an asphalt jump

50:39.317 --> 50:42.698
line, so it's safer and more durable. And then this yellow

50:43.198 --> 50:46.730
line is a paved multi-use path. The section on the right

50:46.971 --> 50:50.184
is existing, but we've extended that to connect to the

50:50.684 --> 50:53.366
Hidden Hollow parking area just across the ridgeline,

50:53.880 --> 50:58.280
since there isn't currently a parking lot on Eagle

50:58.780 --> 51:02.897
Mountain Road. So immediate

51:03.397 --> 51:06.612
next steps for the Mountain Ranch Bike Park, we recommend removing damaged wood

51:07.112 --> 51:09.597
features, reviewing the concept plan and phasing recommendations,

51:10.300 --> 51:14.036
pursuing state and federal grants, and then preparing an

51:14.536 --> 51:18.386
RFP for engineering design. The cost estimate for the total

51:18.419 --> 51:21.612
construction is about $0.9 million to $1.5 million.

51:22.192 --> 51:26.009
That'll be refined through the engineering design. And there

51:26.057 --> 51:29.596
are a lot of state and federal grants available for this type of recreation.

51:32.769 --> 51:35.746
Next chapter, Eagle Mountain NICA Course. So if you're unfamiliar,

51:35.960 --> 51:39.602
this is It stands for the National Interscholastic Cycling

51:39.650 --> 51:43.823
Association. It's mainly a program for middle and high school kids to ride

51:44.323 --> 51:47.429
bikes and mountain bike race. This is

51:47.542 --> 51:50.760
the current course. I know it's a little bit hard to place where

51:51.260 --> 51:54.725
this is, but this is Upper Hidden Valley. What's important here is

51:54.741 --> 51:58.308
just looking at the shape of that course compared

51:58.808 --> 52:01.651
to the next slide, which shows development conflict.

52:02.005 --> 52:05.489
So, This is Mid Valley Road, again shown in red,

52:06.004 --> 52:09.100
and the current NICA course crosses Mid Valley Road in 5

52:09.213 --> 52:12.325
places, which would not be feasible for the NICA

52:12.373 --> 52:16.295
course. So options for that would be either installing

52:16.408 --> 52:20.443
undercrossings or, you know, potentially changing

52:20.943 --> 52:22.791
the alignment of the NICA course. However,

52:24.169 --> 52:27.575
there is a much bigger problem. This one shows development

52:27.640 --> 52:31.250
conflict in yellow. So So you can see most of the NICA course is set

52:31.314 --> 52:34.795
to be developed. We talked with city,

52:35.452 --> 52:37.923
and we came to two solutions for this. Really,

52:39.238 --> 52:42.847
unless you're going to significantly change this master development

52:43.347 --> 52:46.841
agreement, you would have to move the NICA course. So option

52:47.341 --> 52:51.028
one would be relocating to the southeast toward in BLM land. There's a lot

52:51.528 --> 52:55.551
of available land there. Option two is creating a new NICA course near Firefly.

52:55.824 --> 52:58.893
Again, there's a lot of BLM land up there as well. And so

52:59.778 --> 53:03.042
there's still a lot of planning that we need to go into feasibility

53:03.316 --> 53:06.697
and coordinating with NICA, but either of

53:07.197 --> 53:09.533
these options would be a good way to go.

53:12.224 --> 53:16.064
Cameron, just a quick note on that. The Firefly developers have been working

53:16.113 --> 53:19.467
with BLM, and their plan is to create that course,

53:21.209 --> 53:25.088
at least a course that that connects to their downhill trails

53:25.120 --> 53:28.623
and, and with a trailhead and everything. But that

53:28.639 --> 53:32.656
process kind of stalled during the past year or

53:33.156 --> 53:38.126
so as the BLM took some hits on, on nationally,

53:38.239 --> 53:41.934
just on staffing levels. So we hope to revive

53:42.434 --> 53:43.109
that process here soon.

53:45.907 --> 53:49.371
The last chapter here, trailheads and trail signage. So there are

53:49.871 --> 53:53.228
currently 3 main trailheads, the Mountain Ranch Bike Park Trailhead,

53:53.974 --> 53:56.388
Hidden Hollow Trailhead, and Rock City Trailhead.

53:58.825 --> 54:02.361
Rock City is just a gravel parking lot. Hidden Hollow is an actual

54:02.861 --> 54:05.768
parking lot, and Mountain Ranch Bike Park Trailhead is just a road.

54:06.703 --> 54:09.584
We recommend adding 5 additional trailheads.

54:10.507 --> 54:15.557
One, Hidden Hollow Trail Corridor. This would be Southwest

54:15.638 --> 54:19.257
of the Hidden Hollow Trailhead. Jake the Snake Trailhead

54:19.516 --> 54:23.340
in sort of the north area. Firefly Trailhead, Fair and

54:23.373 --> 54:27.251
Square Trailhead, sort of in that middle region, and another

54:27.751 --> 54:31.100
trailhead in the south. Did you guys— so with

54:31.600 --> 54:34.972
the trailheads, what about the Skyline Ridge area?

54:35.037 --> 54:38.957
Because there's parking there in the Skyline.

54:39.551 --> 54:43.308
I think it's in Phase 7 of that. And we own all

54:43.808 --> 54:47.323
the land as a city, and it would end up connecting ultimately to the stuff

54:47.823 --> 54:50.823
that's in Hidden Valley as well. Do you know what I'm talking about, Steve?

54:51.417 --> 54:55.222
I think I do, on that kind of long road that goes into

54:55.833 --> 55:00.007
the hillside there, I think. Yeah, and it's like, there's like a little peninsula

55:00.232 --> 55:04.070
of low land, and then we own everything

55:04.166 --> 55:07.473
up on the cliff as a city. Yeah, I,

55:08.420 --> 55:11.502
I think, um, we can obviously look at that.

55:11.550 --> 55:15.690
I think it's a small, a small trailhead, more of a neighborhood access,

55:16.541 --> 55:19.863
um, because I don't think there's very much parking there. But I

55:19.895 --> 55:23.105
think there's like 5 spots. But you're right, it's not going to be like what

55:23.605 --> 55:27.309
we have in Hidden Valley. It's just if it's part of a trail system,

55:27.759 --> 55:30.985
it makes sense to include something that we required to have

55:31.485 --> 55:34.361
a trailhead parking lot. Yeah, we can show that as a Some sort of minor

55:34.910 --> 55:38.721
neighborhood trailhead or something? Yes, we have in the

55:39.221 --> 55:43.160
plan, we've marked neighborhood access points, which essentially

55:43.660 --> 55:47.001
are trailheads. They would have some sort of map showing you what you're accessing,

55:47.098 --> 55:50.019
but wouldn't have as significant of a parking area.

55:51.266 --> 55:52.480
And I don't have those listed here.

55:54.811 --> 55:58.809
Trail signage, as I mentioned, it's very inconsistent. This left

