01:44:51.371 --> 01:44:54.970
too for people to get upset and say, like, why is the council

01:44:55.002 --> 01:44:58.215
being such sticklers about this? But the reality of it is we look

01:44:58.715 --> 01:45:02.795
at the stability of a neighbourhood. We want each neighborhood to have people

01:45:03.295 --> 01:45:06.394
who wanna live there. And if they can't park their car in certain places

01:45:06.894 --> 01:45:10.172
and they can't do all of those things, people only live there for 2,

01:45:10.188 --> 01:45:13.263
3, 4 years and then they're gone. And then when you have a

01:45:13.763 --> 01:45:16.354
neighborhood that's like transitory like that, where it's constantly changing,

01:45:16.949 --> 01:45:20.898
they lose the pride in their neighborhood. Like it just, and we

01:45:21.398 --> 01:45:24.592
see it because we have enough neighborhoods in Eagle Mountain that we've seen

01:45:24.608 --> 01:45:27.751
that trajectory and what happens. Yeah. So it's more about

01:45:28.251 --> 01:45:31.829
making sure that this has really good staying power as

01:45:31.941 --> 01:45:36.084
a neighborhood and stability than just garage space.

01:45:36.678 --> 01:45:39.938
But the garage space is a key indicator that we've determined

01:45:39.970 --> 01:45:43.390
as we've gone through stuff. I also think it's really neat that you're looking

01:45:43.406 --> 01:45:46.954
at the market and saying the 3 stories just aren't

01:45:47.454 --> 01:45:50.776
cutting it, people don't want them, and being agile enough to come and propose

01:45:50.808 --> 01:45:55.010
something different. I hope that there's a small

01:45:55.059 --> 01:45:58.330
enough tweak that you can make to the floor plan to be able to get

01:45:58.830 --> 01:46:01.473
it to where it needs to be, because I think that it could be a

01:46:01.973 --> 01:46:05.305
very beautiful neighborhood if we can get those things in place. So I,

01:46:05.321 --> 01:46:08.833
I think it is a very beautiful neighborhood, and I would again invite all you

01:46:09.333 --> 01:46:12.713
guys to go look at it. I think you'll be surprised. It looks

01:46:13.258 --> 01:46:17.075
on the inside more like a parade home than a townhome. I don't think that's

01:46:17.171 --> 01:46:20.538
in question. I mean, I've been to your neighborhood. I don't think that's the issue.

01:46:21.195 --> 01:46:24.866
The issue is about usable space inside. You already know

01:46:25.366 --> 01:46:26.965
where I'm going to go. I'm a real estate agent also, by the way.

01:46:27.446 --> 01:46:30.796
What's that? I'm a real estate agent also. When you have 3 stories, it's smaller.

01:46:31.373 --> 01:46:34.178
That's just the reality of it. When you do a 2-story, generally you have a

01:46:34.678 --> 01:46:38.346
bigger footprint. In this case, bigger footprint, we lost

01:46:38.846 --> 01:46:42.128
our garage in the process of doing that. And we've worked pretty hard

01:46:42.273 --> 01:46:45.927
to make our garage spaces usable. It's a fight that we've been fighting

01:46:46.184 --> 01:46:49.889
everywhere, including at the state to make it so that a 2-car garage actually

01:46:50.389 --> 01:46:53.950
fits the cars that people are driving, not a compact car and the minivan,

01:46:54.142 --> 01:46:57.192
because they can't put an SUV in there or a truck.

01:46:57.898 --> 01:47:00.690
And our city drives trucks and SUVs.

01:47:01.284 --> 01:47:04.061
Yep. Uh, that's what we found as we went and did the research. And so

01:47:04.077 --> 01:47:07.206
we, we've tried to accommodate that to make it so that people can stay where

01:47:07.706 --> 01:47:11.732
they buy. Um, and it's

01:47:11.796 --> 01:47:15.245
