Sunset Flats that's moving forward, so we understand they're moving forward with it. So we're

02:00:20.229 --> 02:00:23.744
coming through the entitlement process because we're the parcel next to it,

02:00:24.289 --> 02:00:27.758
and then they go into receivership. So we're, you know, we don't sign ours until

02:00:28.258 --> 02:00:31.611
2023, which we thought they were coming out. We were talking

02:00:32.111 --> 02:00:35.947
to other developers that have been making offers on Sunset Flats. Then we

02:00:36.011 --> 02:00:39.575
hear now the bank, you After we've now went through, we hear the bank is

02:00:39.623 --> 02:00:42.851
going through a merger and they have to wait for all that merger to happen

02:00:43.351 --> 02:00:47.010
for— and I'm going, okay. Then we've went and met with Ivory on Aviator

02:00:47.510 --> 02:00:51.425
Avenue and they're agreeable to work with us, but they're saying, hey, we won't

02:00:51.505 --> 02:00:55.198
want to sign off on Aviator Avenue until the city gets their bypass

02:00:55.698 --> 02:00:59.437
road taken care of. Once again, that was a requirement of our MDA is

02:00:59.534 --> 02:01:03.371
to put in Aviator Road. Well, Citland and Ivory

02:01:03.403 --> 02:01:06.738
won't sign off on that until the bypass is done. I understand you guys

02:01:06.755 --> 02:01:10.170
have been working with them on a negotiation up there and all the Smith stuff

02:01:10.299 --> 02:01:13.378
that has— when you put the amendment together, it had to be worked out for

02:01:13.878 --> 02:01:16.922
that bypass road. And I don't know where that all sits. I'm just going,

02:01:17.339 --> 02:01:20.803
everything's been in play. It's all being talked about. So we're in the process

02:01:21.303 --> 02:01:24.075
going forward because it's all in play. And as it keeps going in play,

02:01:24.091 --> 02:01:27.667
it's— the pieces are not being completed as they're in play. I mean,

02:01:27.940 --> 02:01:31.080
we would have never thought thought as you come out of COVID that a development,

02:01:31.450 --> 02:01:34.950
the developer would, would go belly up. I mean, they had so many offers,

02:01:35.450 --> 02:01:38.770
we don't understand it, you know. But you do have a developer that sends up

02:01:39.270 --> 02:01:42.310
flats. Who would have thought the bank that takes receivership of it, it's not going

02:01:42.810 --> 02:01:44.630
to build, that they're going to go through a merger? I mean, it's going— our

02:01:45.130 --> 02:01:48.430
timing, we were just going through, we didn't sign this till 2023. So we're 3

02:01:48.930 --> 02:01:52.370
years into this, you know, and here we are. Our MDA expires on July

02:01:52.870 --> 02:01:56.540
24th, 2026. We don't dare move them forward because we have to have our

02:01:57.040 --> 02:02:00.600
MDA before a developer. You know, we're not— when you say developer, we're a landowner.

02:02:01.100 --> 02:02:04.540
We're not a developer. We're the landowner. We have to seek a developer to

02:02:05.040 --> 02:02:08.540
come in. We've been under contract with probably 3 different developers, but they can't move

02:02:09.040 --> 02:02:12.800
forward because of all these different processes. So it's not that we're not out there

02:02:13.300 --> 02:02:16.300
trying to market. I know that you've been in our shoes somewhat when you were

02:02:16.800 --> 02:02:20.120
the mayor in Highland. Put yourself in our shoes. You're saying

02:02:20.620 --> 02:02:22.732
we're going to do it this time. This is now the second time for an

02:02:23.232 --> 02:02:26.393
extension. Why do we believe that it's going to go forward now?

02:02:26.746 --> 02:02:30.038
If you haven't got control of the development of your property,

02:02:30.584 --> 02:02:33.089
why do we believe that it's going to be different now? So one is you

02:02:33.589 --> 02:02:37.456
got Sunset Flats now in. So I now have the access coming off from

02:02:37.729 --> 02:02:41.647
the south end of our parcel. The one thing I don't have control over that

02:02:42.147 --> 02:02:45.581
you guys have control is what gets resolved with SITLA with

02:02:46.081 --> 02:02:49.867
that bypass road. We didn't recognize that Sitla was going to hold that up.

02:02:50.717 --> 02:02:53.831
They're trying to use us as leverage to get you guys to get something done

02:02:54.345 --> 02:02:56.897
because they keep going, well, can you go talk to— to be honest, even your

02:02:57.397 --> 02:02:59.866
own staff, just I'm not trying to chuck your staff under, but your staff wanted

02:03:00.366 --> 02:03:03.734
us to go negotiate with Sitla, try to get Aviator in. You'll use the

02:03:04.234 --> 02:03:07.345
other leverage. And I'm going, okay, I agree. I don't control the

02:03:07.845 --> 02:03:11.117
Sitla situation. You guys have been meeting with them. You have a better idea where

02:03:11.617 --> 02:03:14.388
that's at. I think you are holding up Sitla. SITLA for the Smith situation,

02:03:14.404 --> 02:03:18.090
because I understand in that agreement that that doesn't— can't be finalized

02:03:18.590 --> 02:03:22.433
until that bypass road— you guys know where it's at. I don't think the public

02:03:22.933 --> 02:03:25.510
knows where that's all negotiation. I get it has to be behind closed doors,

02:03:26.647 --> 02:03:29.276
but, you know, so that's— you ask why, why we do the extension.

02:03:30.013 --> 02:03:32.833
I believe that you want Smith to come in, and that agreement will probably get

02:03:33.333 --> 02:03:36.904
put together somehow with that bypass road. And after that, that gets put

02:03:37.404 --> 02:03:40.225
together, then we can kind of Get it all taken care of.

02:03:41.493 --> 02:03:43.870
Well, you're probably not going to love where I'm going to go with this.

02:03:44.127 --> 02:03:46.407
I think that if we're going to give an extension, that we want to have

02:03:46.907 --> 02:03:50.116
some discussions about some of your entitlements then. And that's— and that—

02:03:50.164 --> 02:03:53.022
and then I appreciate that. I'm just going to— I think— I mean,

02:03:53.359 --> 02:03:56.185
look, the reason that that code was written the way that it was is so

02:03:56.685 --> 02:03:59.252
that we have people come in that get their entitlements and get to work.

02:03:59.637 --> 02:04:02.897
We're fine giving entitlements to people, but our expectation is come in

02:04:02.913 --> 02:04:07.006
and build it. Where we have been a city that have allowed a lot

02:04:07.506 --> 02:04:10.970
of land entitlements for decades, and that doesn't serve

02:04:11.018 --> 02:04:14.726
the people of Eagle Mountain well. And so we wrote the code

02:04:15.226 --> 02:04:18.128
in place to say, yes, come get your entitlements,

02:04:18.257 --> 02:04:21.804
we'll work through that process, but get to work and get it built. So I

02:04:22.304 --> 02:04:24.597
think as you— and we can talk through it— I think if you look at

02:04:25.097 --> 02:04:28.737
our development, we did work at the time, we were pretty flexible with the city

02:04:28.770 --> 02:04:32.536
going through. with what the land use was. We have

02:04:33.036 --> 02:04:36.238
some 1-acre lots. We buffer from different things. We have— I

02:04:36.738 --> 02:04:39.796
mean, I think we have a good mixture of lots. I don't know if you

02:04:39.828 --> 02:04:42.151
wouldn't still approve. I think that's the question you have to ask is going,

02:04:42.216 --> 02:04:44.972
if this development came in, would you still approve it?

02:04:45.421 --> 02:04:49.219
And if not, why— what would you want? Because I think that what we have

02:04:49.719 --> 02:04:52.632
as a product is still what should be approved in here in Eagle Mountain,

02:04:52.648 --> 02:04:55.582
and it probably still would be approved. I can give you my opinion Only one

02:04:55.678 --> 02:04:59.355
person on the council. Some things have obviously changed because that

02:04:59.692 --> 02:05:03.385
area was never designed to be what it is now, and as a result,

02:05:03.401 --> 02:05:06.837
we now have our our city hall that's

02:05:07.337 --> 02:05:11.398
going in there, our downtown. We've put architectural restrictions

02:05:11.510 --> 02:05:15.187
in there so it has a specific look and feel to it. We press

02:05:15.687 --> 02:05:18.800
pretty hard with the commercial development that's coming in to abide

02:05:19.300 --> 02:05:22.380
by those same things. For me. I'm looking for those same things, that you become

02:05:22.428 --> 02:05:26.089
a partner in that area of building with architectural

02:05:26.121 --> 02:05:29.622
standards that are going to be— that will fit with what that area

02:05:29.638 --> 02:05:33.026
is so that we look intentional in that area. And when people

02:05:33.526 --> 02:05:37.441
come, that your product that your developer is going to come in and build looks

02:05:37.941 --> 02:05:41.070
like we intentionally did this, that the Lowe's and the Smith's,

02:05:41.600 --> 02:05:46.914
the downtown, the city hall, the library, any of the residences

02:05:47.414 --> 02:05:50.748
that are built around that look like this is an intentional idea that we

02:05:50.828 --> 02:05:54.422
wanted to create an environment for our downtown that was uniquely

02:05:54.694 --> 02:05:57.983
Eagle Mountain. And I think, and I, you know, if you look,

02:05:58.047 --> 02:06:00.518
the neighbor to the north of us is going to have a lot higher density

02:06:00.550 --> 02:06:04.208
than what we have. You know, that's, that's what's your plan. And so I'm going,

02:06:04.544 --> 02:06:07.304
okay, so if that's what you're kind of sharing is, hey, we want to have

02:06:07.804 --> 02:06:11.491
a density situation. I notice I didn't say density. I know, but I

02:06:11.539 --> 02:06:15.681
think we're low further away from your town center where our parcel's at than

02:06:15.937 --> 02:06:19.486
what— I know where your parcel is. That's why I'm just trying to— that's

02:06:19.986 --> 02:06:23.163
why I'm going, I'm not sure how close that— where you're trying to envision

02:06:23.663 --> 02:06:26.069
that. And that's something, but that's something to kind of look at. Yeah,

02:06:26.615 --> 02:06:29.907
so that's my opinion. If I'm going to consider

02:06:30.407 --> 02:06:34.017
doing any adjustment to that and giving them

02:06:34.517 --> 02:06:36.891
more time, I think that we need to renegotiate some of the elements of the

02:06:37.391 --> 02:06:39.335
MDA. Any other questions?

02:06:41.800 --> 02:06:45.365
I think just for context of some of the new people, there were

02:06:45.865 --> 02:06:48.464
a lot of things that came out of this approval, or that this was one

02:06:48.964 --> 02:06:51.884
of the first after we approved the new zone code.

02:06:52.724 --> 02:06:57.258
And I remember one of the things that I really pushed on is under

02:06:57.307 --> 02:07:00.680
the residential code, there are 3 different things, and we were always asked for the

02:07:01.180 --> 02:07:05.123
very highest because that we never got The smaller or

02:07:05.236 --> 02:07:08.924
larger lots. So I said the words third,

02:07:08.956 --> 02:07:12.660
third, and third, meaning use all of those zones, not just always the

02:07:13.160 --> 02:07:16.770
biggest one. And what came back because of what I said was this. So if

02:07:17.270 --> 02:07:20.879
you want to know where I was at for context, and I know these two

02:07:21.379 --> 02:07:24.731
know because we worked really hard on this, that was not my intent.

02:07:24.813 --> 02:07:27.889
My intent is, would we approve this under that zone code? Why would it

02:07:28.389 --> 02:07:32.082
not all be the— what zone is

02:07:32.195 --> 02:07:35.489
it? The— not rural density, the residential

02:07:35.989 --> 02:07:39.640
1. R-1. The R-1, rather than mostly R-3.

02:07:40.997 --> 02:07:44.680
But by land mass, that's where it landed, and it did get approval

02:07:45.762 --> 02:07:49.034
based on the fact that what I asked for was a little bit unclear,

02:07:49.645 --> 02:07:52.895
is land mass, not lot size. And my point was, is why not all

02:07:53.249 --> 02:07:56.991
R-1 instead of any of it. So would

02:07:57.491 --> 02:08:01.065
it get approved that way? Probably. I mean, the products— it's not a bad subdivision.

02:08:02.904 --> 02:08:06.720
My point was, is under the residential zoning, why would it not all be

02:08:07.316 --> 02:08:10.761
R-1? So I don't know. I don't know the answer to

02:08:10.778 --> 02:08:14.188
that, but that's up to these guys now. I changed seats. I also think

02:08:14.688 --> 02:08:18.094
it's important, like, Sunset Flats was approved in either 2016 or

02:08:18.594 --> 02:08:22.235
2017, and It took way longer than

02:08:22.735 --> 02:08:25.552
it should have. There were a lot of issues with it. So like, I do

02:08:25.680 --> 02:08:29.029
give him some credit for that. Like, there are things

02:08:29.529 --> 02:08:32.859
that are out of your control, and yet you're still going through the motions and

02:08:33.356 --> 02:08:36.641
executing the parts that you can do. So I'm—

02:08:37.698 --> 02:08:41.064
to me, it's a little different when a developer comes in or a landowner comes

02:08:41.564 --> 02:08:44.909
in and says that, and they've made meaningful progress

02:08:45.409 --> 02:08:48.873
and they're working through and having these meetings versus the ones that show up right

02:08:48.921 --> 02:08:52.453
before their thing expires and they haven't done a thing on it.

02:08:52.550 --> 02:08:56.548
So, and I also look at, this is a very unique chunk

02:08:57.048 --> 02:09:00.642
of land because it goes all the way up to like the stuff that's over

02:09:01.142 --> 02:09:04.945
by the, the bigger lots by Lake Mountain and stuff like that. And I just

02:09:04.977 --> 02:09:08.686
think that it would be really hard

02:09:08.734 --> 02:09:11.480
to get this to a point where we could approve it again. I think we're

02:09:11.980 --> 02:09:14.814
looking at 6 months to a year if we send them back to the drawing

02:09:14.846 --> 02:09:17.041
board. And I don't know that that's fair either.

02:09:17.506 --> 02:09:20.855
So, um, I'm willing to consider it. I think it would

02:09:21.355 --> 02:09:25.358
be good if there are specific things, Council Member Wood, that you want

02:09:25.743 --> 02:09:30.053
to negotiate in there, like throw them out so that like we

02:09:30.553 --> 02:09:34.364
can tackle them. But I don't want to just say sorry,

02:09:34.396 --> 02:09:37.937
no, because I feel like there has been effort made with this.

02:09:39.446 --> 02:09:42.272
Maybe I could ask that question of the applicant, Mayor. Just,

02:09:42.899 --> 02:09:46.110
Mr. Oster, can you just speak to— it sounds like you've had meetings with different

02:09:46.610 --> 02:09:50.108
parties, but you're still the landowner and you haven't, at least yet,

02:09:50.124 --> 02:09:53.721
partnered with a development company. You've had— have you had a hard time getting someone

02:09:54.221 --> 02:09:57.109
to come in and help you? Well, no, you're actually— your first amendment was actually

02:09:57.609 --> 02:10:00.978
Pulte Homes that was under contract with us that came in for— we

02:10:01.478 --> 02:10:04.655
didn't bring the first amendment. It was Pulte Homes that brought the First Amendment,

02:10:05.282 --> 02:10:08.188
that they extended it. Does that mean— so we actually had a developer that was

02:10:08.688 --> 02:10:11.479
coming in doing the extension to make sure they could get through that time,

02:10:12.025 --> 02:10:15.221
you know. And so Impolte is doing part of Sunset Flats, and we went,

02:10:15.654 --> 02:10:19.347
we saw, you know, you got Century Homes and Impolte Homes. We've talked with

02:10:19.847 --> 02:10:22.928
them. We've talked to D.R. Horton. I mean, we see who's actively there. That's who

02:10:22.944 --> 02:10:26.637
we've been talking with. We've talked with Lennar, you know. All of them have showed

02:10:27.137 --> 02:10:31.004
interest, you know. But we were actually under contract with

02:10:31.504 --> 02:10:33.303
Pulte when he— or last year when he did the extension. So that's why they

02:10:33.803 --> 02:10:37.031
did the extension. I mean, when you ask him, we've been active. And you're no

02:10:37.095 --> 02:10:40.386
longer under contract with him, is that what I'm— No, because we don't— we can't

02:10:40.467 --> 02:10:43.718
move forward. Meaning, some of it was the Bureau of Open Water.

02:10:43.782 --> 02:10:46.224
The water kind of changed some things because we've got to go and try to

02:10:46.724 --> 02:10:49.295
get that put together. But some of it's also, okay,

02:10:50.911 --> 02:10:53.802
Ivory has been friendly to us, meaning that they're talking.

02:10:54.384 --> 02:10:57.781
And I kind of thought, hey, this will get resolved as this gets moving forward.

02:10:57.959 --> 02:11:00.918
Guys, can we keep the discussion up here so that we can hear,

02:11:01.128 --> 02:11:04.847
please? So anyway, I appreciate it. But no,

02:11:05.105 --> 02:11:08.548
so we have been actively doing— I mean, we've met with the staff

02:11:09.048 --> 02:11:12.056
on different things when different roads, you know, projects have been going on.

02:11:12.120 --> 02:11:15.708
We've been actively showing up to your different meetings. It's not that we've been— I

02:11:15.757 --> 02:11:18.895
think some of the concern is you do have developers or landowners

02:11:18.927 --> 02:11:22.068
that come get entitlement and also they disappear. We haven't been one that's disappeared.

02:11:22.356 --> 02:11:25.905
We've been active. We've been here showing up. We— when Pulte

02:11:26.405 --> 02:11:29.052
was here, we were here. We just didn't have them do it. But Dave there

02:11:29.552 --> 02:11:32.745
with Pulte moved it forward. And so, I mean, we've been actively doing it.

02:11:32.793 --> 02:11:35.940
I think you can talk with your staff. We've been involved with your staff and

02:11:36.440 --> 02:11:38.638
talking through different things that come over. And so— Well,

02:11:39.296 --> 02:11:42.250
Mayor, to me it sounds like we should probably pull this and address it in

02:11:42.282 --> 02:11:44.932
the policy section, right? That's what I was just going to say. If it's not

02:11:45.432 --> 02:11:51.125
something that we can solve, is this going to be Okay,

02:11:51.434 --> 02:11:54.682
okay, then I think that's where we're at. So let's, uh, address that

02:11:55.182 --> 02:11:58.247
here in a minute in the 7 o'clock hour. And, uh,

02:11:58.816 --> 02:12:02.000
with that, we do have a couple of things, just, uh,

02:12:02.244 --> 02:12:04.356
some updates to talk about in a closed session.

02:12:05.100 --> 02:12:08.547
So if you guys are open to that, I'll entertain a motion.

02:12:11.957 --> 02:12:15.507
Mayor, I move to adjourn to a closed session for the purpose of discussion of

02:12:15.684 --> 02:12:19.200
pending or reasonably imminent litigation, the character,

02:12:19.216 --> 02:12:22.701
professional competence, or physical or mental health of an

02:12:22.733 --> 02:12:26.490
individual, the purchase, lease, sale, or exchange of real property,

02:12:27.164 --> 02:12:31.179
a proposed development agreement, project proposal, or financing proposal

02:12:31.679 --> 02:12:35.434
related to the development of land owned by the state or political

02:12:35.934 --> 02:12:38.918
subdivision, and/or the deployment of security personnel,

02:12:38.966 --> 02:12:44.360
devices, or systems. Pursuant to Section 52-4205

02:12:44.409 --> 02:12:46.560
of the Utah Code Annotated. Second.

02:12:47.579 --> 02:12:51.122
Okay, we have a motion by Councilmember Huish and a second by Councilmember Wood.

02:12:51.267 --> 02:12:54.620
And we'll start with Councilmember Clark. Yes. Councilmember Wright.

02:12:54.830 --> 02:12:57.875
Yes. Councilmember Whiting. Yes. Councilmember Wood. Yes.

02:12:58.231 --> 02:13:01.967
Councilmember Huish. Yes. Okay, we're adjourned to closed

02:13:02.467 --> 02:13:03.309
session at 6:11.

02:14:20.155 --> 02:14:23.361
Okay. I think everybody's here and— Yes.

02:14:24.190 --> 02:14:27.620
Ready. Before we get started, we're— as is

02:14:28.120 --> 02:14:30.547
our tradition, we'll have Steve offer us a prayer.

02:14:38.717 --> 02:14:42.434
Our Heavenly Father, we're so grateful to gather together

02:14:42.934 --> 02:14:47.477
tonight and discuss the needs of our great city and

02:14:47.977 --> 02:14:51.825
our community. We're thankful for the mayor and city council and

02:14:52.325 --> 02:14:55.576
the staff and everyone, volunteers and others who take time

02:14:56.076 --> 02:15:00.236
out of their lives to, um, to help

02:15:00.268 --> 02:15:04.142
our city and, and to help our residents and businesses.

02:15:04.239 --> 02:15:07.720
And we ask thee to bless our

02:15:08.220 --> 02:15:11.408
decision makers tonight with guidance and bless

02:15:11.585 --> 02:15:15.064
that all those here will be kind

02:15:15.564 --> 02:15:19.497
and courteous and also Blessed for their participation.

02:15:20.826 --> 02:15:24.587
And we're so thankful for this great country that we have to live in,

02:15:25.790 --> 02:15:29.381
and ask you to bless those who protect us and first

02:15:29.881 --> 02:15:32.553
responders here in our city as well. And say that in the name of Jesus

02:15:32.650 --> 02:15:36.380
Christ, amen. All right,

02:15:36.510 --> 02:15:39.744
thank you. I will officially call this meeting to order.

02:15:39.842 --> 02:15:43.383
It is 7:04 on the 7th of July.

02:15:43.545 --> 02:15:48.101
And as before we start the meeting, we will— Vince

02:15:48.601 --> 02:15:49.436
will lead us in the Pledge of Allegiance.

02:15:54.365 --> 02:15:57.912
I pledge allegiance to the flag of the United States of

02:15:58.412 --> 02:16:02.024
America and to the Republic for which it stands,

02:16:02.089 --> 02:16:06.210
one nation under God, indivisible, with liberty

02:16:06.308 --> 02:16:07.330
and justice for all.

02:16:14.340 --> 02:16:16.922
Okay, thank you. Um,

02:16:19.048 --> 02:16:21.500
I'm going to give this just a second.

02:16:23.100 --> 02:16:26.538
First thing, if you're ready, I can skip it, but the information items.

02:16:27.070 --> 02:16:29.895
Ben, we can come back to that if you need a second.

02:16:30.877 --> 02:16:34.132
I'm good. Um, thank you, Mayor. We have several things. Uh,

