in donating to this. I think funding for the project through all those

02:35:57.757 --> 02:36:01.176
routes should be relatively simple. The new UTA

02:36:01.208 --> 02:36:05.093
Park and Ride is directly across Pony Express with a crosswalk

02:36:05.173 --> 02:36:08.608
to the location, so funding for a parking lot won't be

02:36:09.108 --> 02:36:12.445
necessary. I reached out to a local professional course designer

02:36:12.942 --> 02:36:16.282
who can put together a planned-out course concept with projected

02:36:16.298 --> 02:36:20.409
cost estimate that can be bid out to private contractors or installed

02:36:20.909 --> 02:36:24.889
by the designer and his crew, making design and installation simple

02:36:25.389 --> 02:36:28.887
as well. Disc golf as a sport is growing at an extremely

02:36:28.903 --> 02:36:32.515
fast rate, and giving players near and far a reason to come to

02:36:32.612 --> 02:36:35.470
Eagle Mountain helps the sport and it helps the city.

02:36:35.807 --> 02:36:36.305
Thank you. Thank you.

02:36:39.932 --> 02:36:41.192
Bronwyn Richardson.

02:36:47.745 --> 02:36:51.061
Hi guys, my name is Bronwyn Richardson. I am a full-time student and

02:36:51.561 --> 02:36:55.223
a stay-at-home mom, and I have proudly called Eagle Mountain home for nearly

02:36:55.723 --> 02:36:59.050
10 years. When I learned that this council is proposing

02:36:59.098 --> 02:37:02.978
a 220% increase in this— in the city's

02:37:03.444 --> 02:37:06.306
property tax rate, I was shocked.

02:37:06.869 --> 02:37:10.260
I don't believe this proposal reflects the financial reality of

02:37:10.760 --> 02:37:13.796
many families in our community face.

02:37:15.490 --> 02:37:18.985
According to the latest Title I designations from Alpine School District,

02:37:19.406 --> 02:37:23.937
Eagle Mountain has 2 Title I elementary

02:37:24.437 --> 02:37:27.042
schools and a Title I preschool.

02:37:28.343 --> 02:37:31.976
Title I status exists because a significant number

02:37:32.476 --> 02:37:34.486
of students come from low-income households.

02:37:35.730 --> 02:37:39.417
And that should remind us all that not every family

02:37:39.449 --> 02:37:42.851
in Eagle Mountain is thriving financially. Some families

02:37:43.351 --> 02:37:46.756
in our city struggle to afford food, clothing, housing,

02:37:47.498 --> 02:37:50.306
and other basic necessities. For those families,

02:37:50.419 --> 02:37:53.149
another $30 a month isn't just $30.

02:37:54.638 --> 02:37:58.166
It's another bill in a budget that's already stretched to its

02:37:58.666 --> 02:38:02.616
limit. Property taxes aren't optional. Families can't

02:38:03.116 --> 02:38:06.303
decide to skip paying them or wait until

02:38:06.803 --> 02:38:10.538
next month. Every increase falls hardest on those with

02:38:10.634 --> 02:38:14.013
the least flexibility. The city has stated

02:38:14.353 --> 02:38:17.970
that this proposal would generate approximately $6.9 million,

02:38:19.596 --> 02:38:23.011
um, in additional revenue. Before asking residents

02:38:23.075 --> 02:38:26.573
to provide that much more in taxes, I ask

02:38:26.746 --> 02:38:30.017
you first consider the burden being placed upon

02:38:30.049 --> 02:38:33.064
the, upon the people who earn that money.

02:38:34.139 --> 02:38:38.292
The median household income in Eagle Mountain is $113,000,

02:38:38.774 --> 02:38:42.526
yet housing costs remain high. Average rents exceed

02:38:42.622 --> 02:38:46.215
$2,000 a month, and families continue to face

02:38:46.391 --> 02:38:49.454
rising costs for groceries, fuel, insurance,

02:38:49.839 --> 02:38:53.760
and child care. Even households earning the median

02:38:53.873 --> 02:38:57.022
income are feeling the pressure. I'm also asking for

02:38:57.522 --> 02:39:01.307
greater transparency. The city's Truth in Taxation information states that

02:39:01.339 --> 02:39:04.809
100% of the additional revenue will

02:39:04.841 --> 02:39:08.470
go towards public safety. However, residents have

02:39:08.649 --> 02:39:12.199
also heard public statements discussing employees' compensation as

02:39:12.231 --> 02:39:15.165
part of this proposal. Both statements are accurate.

02:39:16.150 --> 02:39:20.143
If both statements are accurate, then please provide— Order. A clear— 30 seconds remaining.

02:39:20.320 --> 02:39:24.016
Line-by-line statement

02:39:24.209 --> 02:39:27.970
accounting, showing exactly for how every

02:39:28.470 --> 02:39:32.929
additional tax dollar will be spent. And I will close with

02:39:33.429 --> 02:39:37.287
a quote from Edmund Burke: Overtaxation cost England her 13 American

02:39:37.787 --> 02:39:38.188
colonies. Thank you.

02:39:42.033 --> 02:39:42.822
Finally, Jeff Ruth.

02:40:09.302 --> 02:40:12.836
Jeff Earth from the Landing neighborhood. I understand

02:40:12.900 --> 02:40:15.719
parking's going to be on the agenda tonight,

02:40:16.509 --> 02:40:18.603
and I talked with Jim a little bit about it.

02:40:20.590 --> 02:40:24.890
I hope you pass it, because what you see right there is

02:40:25.390 --> 02:40:28.415
the neighborhood kids just keep adding cars.

02:40:29.417 --> 02:40:33.185
The farthest one there hasn't

02:40:33.685 --> 02:40:37.083
moved in probably a year. The other one's been several months. Thank you. Then they

02:40:37.165 --> 02:40:40.805
added 2 more, and then here a couple weeks ago they added one sitting on

02:40:40.903 --> 02:40:44.419
the street with a car cover. I had

02:40:44.919 --> 02:40:48.447
some dogs coming in my yard, so I went and drove around trying to see

02:40:48.463 --> 02:40:52.184
where they were coming from, and I couldn't believe how

02:40:52.684 --> 02:40:56.254
the alleys looked like a ghetto because

02:40:56.286 --> 02:40:59.644
everybody just puts their junk back there in the alleys and

02:40:59.660 --> 02:41:03.642
lets it build up. Well, they can't get a car in. Their driveway or

02:41:04.142 --> 02:41:07.353
in the back. So what do they do? They just keep parking more on the

02:41:07.401 --> 02:41:10.983
roads. So I hope you pass it. I mean,

02:41:11.898 --> 02:41:15.320
Neighborhood Improvement has a tough job, but I sure do appreciate

02:41:15.820 --> 02:41:19.753
them. Um, and like I said, this is just one example.

02:41:20.187 --> 02:41:23.431
If you drive through the alleys in my neighborhood, it's bad.

02:41:24.251 --> 02:41:27.608
And Neighborhood Improvement brought down a couple of dumpsters to

02:41:27.624 --> 02:41:30.867
try to get people to clean up. I'll admit I went in

02:41:31.367 --> 02:41:34.448
my basement and I was amazed at how much junk I took out of there.

02:41:34.930 --> 02:41:37.948
So there again, I thank Neighborhood Improvement for helping.

02:41:39.185 --> 02:41:42.444
Um, so please move it forward.

02:41:42.717 --> 02:41:45.848
Let Neighborhood Improvement take care of some of

02:41:46.348 --> 02:41:49.332
these cars and junk that's sitting around. Um,

02:41:50.745 --> 02:41:53.090
and just one thing on the tax proposal.

02:41:54.137 --> 02:41:57.682
If it's all going towards law enforcement

02:41:58.182 --> 02:42:01.679
and fire, I fully support it. I mean, from somebody that

02:42:02.179 --> 02:42:06.217
struggles month to month, there's been many times I've called

02:42:06.635 --> 02:42:10.412
officers out and I never saw them. So I

02:42:10.912 --> 02:42:14.155
believe it's something we need. So thank you all for all you do. Thank you.

02:42:18.901 --> 02:42:22.667
Okay, that's all the forms we have turned in. Just last call, is there anyone

02:42:23.167 --> 02:42:25.343
else here that would like to address the council that did not get a chance?

02:42:27.726 --> 02:42:30.636
See, okay, go ahead and come on up, and I'll just ask you, if you

02:42:31.136 --> 02:42:32.831
didn't get a chance to fill out the form, to do it after and return

02:42:33.331 --> 02:42:37.482
it to Lacey. And state your name for the record, please. Sarah Davenport-Smith,

02:42:37.579 --> 02:42:41.182
and actually I'm a resident of Saratoga Springs, but I come to Eagle

02:42:41.682 --> 02:42:44.974
Mountain a lot. Rotary Club International, I just wanted to say

02:42:45.474 --> 02:42:48.626
a little bit about us because Bill didn't get to tell you about us.

02:42:49.284 --> 02:42:52.003
He talked about Mare Gray a lot, which was super fun.

02:42:53.380 --> 02:42:56.358
But I just wanted to let you guys know that Rotary International has been around

02:42:56.406 --> 02:42:59.805
since the early 1900s, and it was started

02:42:59.886 --> 02:43:03.385
by a group of businessmen in Middle America,

02:43:03.677 --> 02:43:07.439
and they said, you know, we want to do something for our community. They happened

02:43:07.939 --> 02:43:10.170
to be in Chicago, and they said, what do we do?

02:43:10.655 --> 02:43:14.588
Well, they built some infrastructure. They built the first public toilet

02:43:15.088 --> 02:43:18.197
in the city. That's amazing. That area. And from there, it's grown.

02:43:18.662 --> 02:43:20.878
And in fact, you may or may not know,

02:43:21.472 --> 02:43:25.438
Rotary International is mainly responsible for

02:43:25.888 --> 02:43:29.452
getting vaccines to a lot of children

02:43:29.565 --> 02:43:32.969
around the world, and polio is almost gone

02:43:33.033 --> 02:43:36.646
because of the efforts of Rotary. But we do a lot,

02:43:37.111 --> 02:43:40.467
and one of the other things that I get to be in charge of is

02:43:40.596 --> 02:43:43.535
our youth. And also our youth exchange.

02:43:44.117 --> 02:43:47.998
We have the best worldwide student exchange

02:43:48.014 --> 02:43:51.793
program, and it's fully supported by our State

02:43:52.293 --> 02:43:56.062
Department as well. And in fact, we have one child

02:43:56.562 --> 02:43:59.735
heading off to Belgium, another one who

02:43:59.751 --> 02:44:03.655
is from Belgium, and someone who is going to South America

02:44:04.155 --> 02:44:07.683
this summer. And that's all just from our little club in Lehi. and clubs

02:44:07.860 --> 02:44:11.344
all around Utah. Rotary Clubs do that all around Utah,

02:44:11.408 --> 02:44:14.812
but we do a lot. The reason why the mayor got to play in

02:44:14.844 --> 02:44:19.067
our golf tournament was to support our scholarship program, which supports

02:44:19.999 --> 02:44:23.932
Westlake, Lehi High School, and also

02:44:24.880 --> 02:44:29.424
Sky Ridge. And we would love to include Cedar

02:44:29.552 --> 02:44:32.965
as well. We would love to include Eagle Mountain. So— Thank you. Anyways, just wanted

02:44:33.465 --> 02:44:36.129
to let you guys know about who we are and what we do and why

02:44:36.629 --> 02:44:40.032
Rotary is awesome. Thanks a lot. Hey, hold on, I got to— you

02:44:40.532 --> 02:44:42.660
got to say something for me. Okay. I wrote it down, and one of the

02:44:43.160 --> 02:44:46.838
reasons these recognitions were, uh, put the way they were is

02:44:47.113 --> 02:44:50.494
the I believe in you and that kind of attitude of giving back.

02:44:50.899 --> 02:44:53.655
What's the motto? Rotary?

02:44:54.077 --> 02:44:57.596
Rotary International? Um, service above self. There you go.

02:44:57.746 --> 02:45:01.143
I wrote it down, I hear service before self. And I think that's part

02:45:01.643 --> 02:45:04.186
of the message that we were talking about earlier is that, you know, you get

02:45:04.686 --> 02:45:07.713
a lot more than— if you give, you get back more than you give.

02:45:08.375 --> 02:45:10.601
And so I just love that.

02:45:12.181 --> 02:45:15.262
I've given a few speeches and I use that all the time, service before self.

02:45:15.407 --> 02:45:18.285
So yeah, thank you. Thank you.

02:45:19.129 --> 02:45:21.935
Was there anyone else that did not get a chance to speak? Don't forget to

02:45:21.951 --> 02:45:25.501
fill out a form if you would. Seeing none,

02:45:25.776 --> 02:45:29.177
we will close the public hearing, the public comment portion,

02:45:29.744 --> 02:45:33.326
and move on to our next item, which is council and mayor items.

02:45:34.008 --> 02:45:36.865
We're going to start on this side with Melissa.

02:45:38.720 --> 02:45:41.952
Thank you, Mayor. I'm grateful that so many people came out, and I know that

02:45:42.534 --> 02:45:45.993
you all have different things that you're interested in,

02:45:46.171 --> 02:45:49.565
and I love the texts from our residents, and I love the messages

02:45:49.828 --> 02:45:53.620
and the information

02:45:53.652 --> 02:45:55.477
that we wouldn't otherwise have without your help.

02:45:57.309 --> 02:46:00.592
We had some elections for our Youth

02:46:00.689 --> 02:46:03.892
Council mayor this last week, and there were 3

02:46:03.924 --> 02:46:07.170
great candidates, right, Councilmember Whiting? Yeah.

02:46:07.379 --> 02:46:10.657
And I had the opportunity to just talk with Councilmember Whiting today,

02:46:10.673 --> 02:46:14.032
and we were talking about how sometimes it's hard when really amazing

02:46:14.082 --> 02:46:18.926
people don't end up getting elected. And I told

02:46:19.426 --> 02:46:22.199
him, I said, in a lot of ways, it's like when Mayor Gray and I

02:46:22.312 --> 02:46:25.440
were running against each other. Now, what you guys don't know is we're really good

02:46:25.940 --> 02:46:29.451
friends, so everything's great. But after the election,

02:46:29.644 --> 02:46:33.365
I had a choice. I could withdraw and just keep

02:46:33.865 --> 02:46:37.225
to myself and not serve, or I could dig in and support Mayor

02:46:37.257 --> 02:46:40.329
Gray and show up for our city every single day.

02:46:40.979 --> 02:46:43.914
And I chose to do that. I'm glad that that's what I chose. So I

02:46:44.414 --> 02:46:48.145
encourage the 2 individuals that didn't get elected to

02:46:48.645 --> 02:46:52.103
choose that option because we need you. We need you on our Youth

02:46:52.151 --> 02:46:56.158
Council regardless of what your title is. We need you, uh, part of

02:46:56.658 --> 02:47:00.468
our city regardless of what your role is here. You're amazing

02:47:00.484 --> 02:47:03.770
and we're grateful for all that you bring. Um, the other thing that

02:47:03.850 --> 02:47:08.003
I wanted to say is I'm incredibly proud of our city I'm

02:47:08.439 --> 02:47:12.201
proud of our events. Like, you guys, we had the most epic fireworks

02:47:12.782 --> 02:47:16.253
display ever, and I'm so proud of the people of our community

02:47:16.350 --> 02:47:20.563
that rallied behind the request to

02:47:21.063 --> 02:47:24.195
not use fireworks during this kind of crazy year of drought.

02:47:24.808 --> 02:47:27.494
And I'm also incredibly proud of our staff.

02:47:29.099 --> 02:47:32.437
Over the weekend, we had some well issues, and we

02:47:32.937 --> 02:47:36.285
came to you and we said, Hey, could you guys not water? And we reached

02:47:36.785 --> 02:47:39.030
out to the schools and the churches and all of those things, and we asked

02:47:39.530 --> 02:47:43.077
them to also not water just so we could keep our tanks at the level

02:47:43.109 --> 02:47:45.967
they need to stay at. So one, we could fight fires, and two, we wouldn't

02:47:45.999 --> 02:47:49.435
have to— it wouldn't drop below the PSI so we'd have to do a boil

02:47:49.628 --> 02:47:53.931
order. And in addition to getting

02:47:54.429 --> 02:47:58.122
on the fixes this week and taking care of some of the issues with

02:47:58.622 --> 02:48:02.919
the 2 wells that went down, We had communications staff that was like basically

02:48:02.935 --> 02:48:06.063
on call all weekend long giving updates, sending out texts,

02:48:06.079 --> 02:48:10.427
doing all of that stuff. We had water and everyone

02:48:10.491 --> 02:48:14.181
basically from Public Works stepping in to help. And crazy

02:48:14.245 --> 02:48:17.405
things are happening at 1 o'clock in the morning. And I'm

02:48:17.905 --> 02:48:22.073
incredibly proud of the men and women who come and give

02:48:22.137 --> 02:48:25.490
so much of their time here in the city, and they don't make a ton

02:48:25.990 --> 02:48:29.117
of money. Um, but they keep coming back because they're crazy,

02:48:29.566 --> 02:48:33.499
and we're super grateful for all that they do. And I just want the residents

02:48:33.999 --> 02:48:38.699
to know that yes, we are looking at this tax rate raise,

02:48:38.811 --> 02:48:42.583
and we need to have our public truth in taxation in our public

02:48:43.083 --> 02:48:47.044
hearing. But I promise you that the people who are sitting

02:48:47.060 --> 02:48:50.511
up here, when we look at what the budget is and

02:48:50.688 --> 02:48:54.935
dig down into every single line of the budget. We think about

02:48:55.435 --> 02:48:58.946
the Jeff Ruths of our community, and we think about the Bronwins.

02:48:59.606 --> 02:49:02.810
You matter to us, and we know how financially

02:49:02.988 --> 02:49:06.433
hard the situation is right now for all of us.

02:49:07.254 --> 02:49:10.555
And please know that we're being very careful

02:49:11.055 --> 02:49:14.292
about the decisions we're making, and we're grateful for what you bring to our

02:49:14.792 --> 02:49:15.765
community. So thank you for being here.

02:49:19.557 --> 02:49:22.482
I'm just gonna say ditto. That's pretty easy to say ditto to.

02:49:24.848 --> 02:49:28.427
I was just— so on the 4th of July, I found myself alone

02:49:28.689 --> 02:49:32.713
in the park. My wife wasn't feeling well. My family, we had an

02:49:32.729 --> 02:49:36.216
event scheduled yesterday, so I had all 9 of my grandchildren and my 4 children

02:49:36.312 --> 02:49:39.623
who were here. So I had some— I had one son and his wife and

02:49:40.123 --> 02:49:45.276
3 grandchildren come in from out of state. And so I—

02:49:46.439 --> 02:49:48.943
Those of you that know me, I can get a little booby, so I'm sorry

02:49:49.443 --> 02:49:52.286
about that. I might just cry about this. But just sitting in the park alone,

02:49:53.757 --> 02:49:56.906
I had a couple of residents who sat by me who recognized me, and we

02:49:57.406 --> 02:50:00.810
had some conversations about what was happening. It was a pretty remarkable

02:50:00.842 --> 02:50:03.878
experience to spend 2 and a half hours just observing.

02:50:04.473 --> 02:50:07.783
I saw Jacob there. He came to the fireworks show and said hello

02:50:08.283 --> 02:50:11.886
to him and some of his children. children, and several other residents

02:50:12.386 --> 02:50:15.705
who I recognized and spent some time with. I just don't think—

02:50:16.881 --> 02:50:21.891
I don't

02:50:21.972 --> 02:50:25.994
think our average citizens understand

02:50:26.494 --> 02:50:29.894
the level of commitment that comes from these individuals who

02:50:30.394 --> 02:50:30.799
sit here. We care.

02:50:33.857 --> 02:50:37.786
Sitting there looking at the 16,700 people that came to that event, on the

02:50:38.286 --> 02:50:43.308
2-hour block and watching the fireworks and just feeling overwhelmed

02:50:43.808 --> 02:50:46.144
with the quality of work that our staff produced,

02:50:47.336 --> 02:50:50.218
watching the interactions of our citizens one with another,

02:50:50.975 --> 02:50:54.678
celebrating what has to be one of the most magnificent holidays that

02:50:55.178 --> 02:50:58.427
we're going to experience this year, the 250th anniversary of the birth of this nation.

02:51:00.281 --> 02:51:00.654
And, uh,

02:51:06.107 --> 02:51:12.904
I just felt gratitude to live

02:51:13.404 --> 02:51:16.684
in a country where we're free, to have an opportunity to serve,

02:51:19.852 --> 02:51:23.603
and humbled with this question that kept resonating

02:51:23.635 --> 02:51:26.984
in my mind: who are you, Brad, to sit in

02:51:27.017 --> 02:51:31.046
this seat and to make decisions for the 80,000 people that are impacted

02:51:31.546 --> 02:51:34.994
by those decisions, recognize that I feel that. And so when we're talking about a

02:51:35.494 --> 02:51:37.926
tax increase, oh goodness gracious, that's hard.

02:51:38.860 --> 02:51:42.365
My 80-year-old mother lives in the city, my 35-year-old daughter lives

02:51:42.865 --> 02:51:45.884
in the city, my 38-year-old son lives in the city, and their families are impacted

02:51:45.916 --> 02:51:49.532
by it. My wife and I live here, and I have numerous other family members

02:51:50.032 --> 02:51:54.329
and friends who live here. And I

02:51:54.361 --> 02:51:57.623
wish the tax increases had come $2 at a time over the last 10 years.

02:51:57.688 --> 02:52:00.802
That's what I really wish had happened rather than a $26 or $27

02:52:00.914 --> 02:52:04.397
a month adjustment. But as we've dug into this and looked at

02:52:04.897 --> 02:52:08.715
this, this is for public safety. All of those resources, that $6.9 million

02:52:08.955 --> 02:52:11.764
that you're referencing, is going to go into the public safety fund. It's going to

02:52:12.264 --> 02:52:16.338
pay for our sheriff's contract. What you probably don't understand, and the nuance

02:52:16.838 --> 02:52:19.773
to that is, is that we have been using other resources to fund our sheriff's

02:52:20.273 --> 02:52:23.657
department. We've only had about $2.8 million that has been dedicated to that

02:52:24.157 --> 02:52:27.928
from property tax. And so that shortfall has been coming out of sales tax

02:52:27.976 --> 02:52:31.589
and other fees. So what does that mean to you as residents? Here's what it

02:52:32.089 --> 02:52:35.619
means. It means your parks haven't been taken care of. It means that the public

02:52:36.119 --> 02:52:39.585
spaces aren't being taken care of. It means we're not able to build roads as

02:52:39.617 --> 02:52:42.700
quickly and as often as we'd like to, because boy, do we get beat up

02:52:42.764 --> 02:52:46.361
about those things. And I'm looking at Jeff, because Jeff comes and he

02:52:46.441 --> 02:52:49.765
beats us up pretty good sometimes, and we love him. But he's fair.

02:52:50.070 --> 02:52:53.185
I'm just gonna say that. He's fair in his assessments. So I want you to

02:52:53.685 --> 02:52:57.264
know that this is an opportunity. I'm really grateful for the

02:52:57.764 --> 02:53:01.551
opportunity to serve you. And there is nothing that we do here that

02:53:01.599 --> 02:53:03.606
is easy or that is automatic.

02:53:04.505 --> 02:53:07.556
Everything that we contemplate is weighted.

02:53:08.583 --> 02:53:11.907
And I just am grateful that you've extended me that opportunity.

02:53:12.052 --> 02:53:13.352
So thank you. Thank you.

02:53:16.532 --> 02:53:20.241
You can see why it's easy to serve with these fellow city

02:53:20.741 --> 02:53:23.243
council members. I love all of them, and I'm grateful for their, for their service

02:53:23.291 --> 02:53:26.984
and their wisdom and what they do to make this city a great

02:53:27.484 --> 02:53:31.223
place. And I just wanted to extend a gratitude

02:53:31.239 --> 02:53:35.013
for Jesse and Angeline for taking the

02:53:36.281 --> 02:53:39.509
role to serve again as youth city council advisor

02:53:40.009 --> 02:53:42.264
for the coming year. And I know it takes a lot of time out of

02:53:42.764 --> 02:53:46.283
your personal schedule to help with that capacity, but it is

02:53:46.315 --> 02:53:49.535
such a significant one. Eagle Mountain

02:53:50.035 --> 02:53:53.488
City's got the greatest youth and they deserve the greatest programs

02:53:53.988 --> 02:53:57.329
around them, and grateful for this. And a lot of people don't know, but our

02:53:57.426 --> 02:54:01.081
Youth City Council has, I think, tripled in size basically since last

02:54:01.581 --> 02:54:04.669
year. And a lot of that's because of what's going on with the advisors,

02:54:04.685 --> 02:54:08.300
and we're appreciative for you're doing. The youth are our

02:54:08.348 --> 02:54:12.623
future, and we need to make sure this city provides the resources to

02:54:12.912 --> 02:54:16.758
make it the best youth city in the state. So thank

02:54:17.258 --> 02:54:21.462
you for your service. And I likewise just comment

02:54:21.510 --> 02:54:24.972
that it is a weighty seat that

02:54:25.472 --> 02:54:29.330
we sit in. And even though we respect one

02:54:29.830 --> 02:54:32.735
another here on the dais a lot, We, we disagree.

02:54:32.816 --> 02:54:36.701
And if you stay tonight, you're going to see probably 3 or 4 disagreements,

02:54:36.974 --> 02:54:40.009
like, like different agenda items where there'll be a lot of discussion.

02:54:40.442 --> 02:54:43.798
And that's healthy. And that's— and the great thing is we can discuss our

02:54:44.298 --> 02:54:47.732
differences of opinion and still hold respect for one another. And, and really, that should,

02:54:47.829 --> 02:54:51.714
should be the clarion call for any of us who, who discuss politics

02:54:51.762 --> 02:54:55.664
and different ideas one with another, that we can, we can do so understanding

02:54:55.696 --> 02:54:59.443
that there's a genuine love between those who are discussing, and that it's

02:54:59.492 --> 02:55:03.208
okay for disagreement to occur and have differences of opinion. But thank

02:55:03.708 --> 02:55:04.929
you for giving us the opportunity to serve you.

02:55:07.017 --> 02:55:09.773
And we live in an awesome city, and I'm grateful for the staff, grateful for

02:55:10.273 --> 02:55:13.647
the city council members, grateful for our great mayor. And thank you again for giving

02:55:13.679 --> 02:55:14.490
us the chance to serve.

02:55:17.050 --> 02:55:19.526
First of all, grateful to see so many people out here.

02:55:20.218 --> 02:55:23.017
I love when there's— the seats are filled and we get to hear from more.

02:55:23.113 --> 02:55:26.642
I wish more people would have given more public comment. I actually want to address

02:55:27.142 --> 02:55:31.283
Bronwyn, if that's okay. Um, I realized—

02:55:31.652 --> 02:55:34.622
so I've been trying to stay off social media lately because I just feel like

02:55:34.671 --> 02:55:37.786
it's super divisive. Uh, just a lot of,

02:55:37.898 --> 02:55:41.479
uh, a lot of people that just want to watch the world burn.

02:55:42.378 --> 02:55:45.926
This last week, as I was thinking about my responsibilities on the city

02:55:46.426 --> 02:55:50.149
council, I actually took Craig's advice and started watching some

02:55:50.900 --> 02:55:52.923
documentaries about the forefathers.

02:55:54.882 --> 02:55:57.682
Thomas Jefferson was one of them that made me think about this.

02:55:58.216 --> 02:56:00.693
I can't remember what it was that I was going to write it down.

02:56:00.806 --> 02:56:03.797
It's a great quote. But what it made me do is it made me look

02:56:03.862 --> 02:56:07.612
differently at what was being said by citizens that were

02:56:08.162 --> 02:56:11.936
upset about some of the decisions. And there was 3 major decisions that

02:56:12.436 --> 02:56:14.270
people were upset about in the last week and a half.

02:56:15.735 --> 02:56:20.218
Decision about the fireworks. Decision about turning

02:56:20.235 --> 02:56:23.524
the water off in your yards and not watering

02:56:24.024 --> 02:56:27.246
so that we could fill our tanks back up, and obviously the tax.

02:56:28.353 --> 02:56:31.706
This is what I figured out: I think we think we're

02:56:31.722 --> 02:56:35.236
doing a great job of getting the messaging out, and I realized

02:56:35.736 --> 02:56:39.503
from your comment today that it's not—that you're not

02:56:40.003 --> 02:56:42.439
hearing what we're saying. We're not saying it in a way that it's being understood.

02:56:42.596 --> 02:56:47.461
Understood. For that, I want to apologize. It's not intentional.

02:56:47.688 --> 02:56:51.147
We feel the sting like everybody else does. We pay the same taxes that everybody

02:56:51.244 --> 02:56:54.577
else does. And I actually wanted to just make one caveat to

02:56:54.625 --> 02:56:57.918
what Brett said so that it is clear: there's

02:56:57.934 --> 02:57:01.308
not extra money when we

02:57:01.808 --> 02:57:05.471
do the the tax for the property. It's all going to

02:57:05.487 --> 02:57:08.330
pay for our entire contract, not new.

02:57:08.746 --> 02:57:12.779
People our entire contract with the Sheriff's Department. And the reason

02:57:13.279 --> 02:57:16.395
that that decision was made is because everybody uses that service,

02:57:16.877 --> 02:57:20.428
and it's a fixed cost that raises as we add more people to

02:57:20.928 --> 02:57:24.252
our city. They all need to have public safety. All of

02:57:24.752 --> 02:57:27.803
our other funding sources fluctuate, and we

02:57:28.303 --> 02:57:30.792
can't afford to have that one fluctuate. That would— that needs to be steady.

02:57:31.821 --> 02:57:35.434
To his comment about the other sources of income

02:57:35.934 --> 02:57:39.094
from sales tax and other sources. There are a lot of other things that we

02:57:39.402 --> 02:57:42.213
don't have the money to pay for that we need to pay for. And we

02:57:42.713 --> 02:57:46.238
have been running— I've said this

02:57:46.738 --> 02:57:51.391
before, and I know it hasn't been popular with previous councils— we've

02:57:51.891 --> 02:57:54.920
been running cheaply, not conservatively. And as a result,

02:57:54.953 --> 02:57:58.451
we're paying as citizens in Eagle Mountain 2026,

02:57:58.499 --> 02:58:01.945
we're paying for decisions not to do a steady increase so

02:58:01.993 --> 02:58:05.028
that we could have the same services that are being afforded in other cities.

02:58:06.023 --> 02:58:09.973
And this council decided enough is enough. And we recognized that

02:58:10.021 --> 02:58:13.666
we were going to be taking the heat for past decisions so that we could

02:58:14.166 --> 02:58:17.793
make things right. And that's happened with fees for utilities.

02:58:18.547 --> 02:58:21.389
It has happened with— and by the way, I feel all of these things too.

02:58:21.598 --> 02:58:25.114
It's put a hurt on our family's finances to pay more for our sewer and

02:58:25.614 --> 02:58:29.684
water. I feel that, and the taxes going up, I will feel that as well.

02:58:31.251 --> 02:58:34.633
At the end of the day, what is best for everybody

02:58:34.827 --> 02:58:38.383
is that we make sure we can we can pay our bills, that we're

02:58:38.883 --> 02:58:42.021
providing the services that you should expect in other cities as well, and that we're

02:58:42.521 --> 02:58:45.427
providing a quality of life that you come to expect.

02:58:46.202 --> 02:58:50.235
I I hope that we can do better in expressing that in the

02:58:50.735 --> 02:58:53.513
message that we're giving to people as we talk about something that is. Always going

02:58:53.545 --> 02:58:57.160
to be divisive, like talking about taxes. Because I think if

02:58:57.660 --> 02:59:01.372
you knew all the people up here directly, we've had very heated debates about

02:59:01.872 --> 02:59:03.391
whether we should do any tax increases.

02:59:05.237 --> 02:59:08.515
At the end of the day, what it came down to is that how else

02:59:09.015 --> 02:59:12.786
are you going to pay for the essential services? And that's the

02:59:12.802 --> 02:59:17.747
end result of those discussions. So thank

02:59:18.247 --> 02:59:21.789
you for bringing that to our attention. We will do better in getting that information

02:59:21.837 --> 02:59:24.988
out so that people understand we know it's

02:59:25.488 --> 02:59:28.124
hard, we're sorry, but there is no other way.

02:59:29.646 --> 02:59:33.272
The other thing that I wanted to just make comment— I'm extremely

02:59:33.288 --> 02:59:36.623
grateful for the efforts that our staff has been putting

02:59:37.123 --> 02:59:40.564
in, particularly our event staff, over the last couple of months.

02:59:42.034 --> 02:59:45.941
I love Ben's message because I think it emulates What

02:59:46.390 --> 02:59:50.019
I feel like Eagle Mountain's way is. Our way is to believe

02:59:50.519 --> 02:59:52.957
in each other, that we believe in community.

02:59:53.888 --> 02:59:58.191
I'm grateful for the people who are dedicating their time as people

02:59:58.207 --> 03:00:01.836
that are sitting on boards, people that are doing that are elected

03:00:02.336 --> 03:00:05.898
officials, our first responders, and even the people who it's their job

03:00:06.398 --> 03:00:09.783
to be here. It's still a sacrifice. It's not easy being a

03:00:10.283 --> 03:00:13.480
member of a city hall. Because there's a lot of times that people are not

03:00:13.594 --> 03:00:17.027
very forgiving when the decision wasn't really yours to make.

03:00:17.091 --> 03:00:20.443
You're just the one who gets to hear it, and I want them to know

03:00:20.460 --> 03:00:23.714
I appreciate that they that they do those things, that they are

03:00:24.214 --> 03:00:27.923
taking it for the team. I recognize

03:00:28.423 --> 03:00:31.669
that. I know it's not easy. I'm asking for our citizens to

03:00:32.169 --> 03:00:35.244
be a little more forgiving, give a little more grace,

03:00:36.344 --> 03:00:40.332
recognize that. The people in, in City Hall, your elected officials,

03:00:40.445 --> 03:00:43.891
the people who are serving on boards are doing their best, and they're doing

03:00:43.939 --> 03:00:47.023
the best with the information that they have. Thank you for being here.

03:00:47.120 --> 03:00:50.401
I'm extremely proud to be a member of this community, very,

03:00:50.499 --> 03:00:53.033
very proud to be an American, especially this last week.

03:00:53.666 --> 03:00:54.218
Thanks for coming.

03:00:57.561 --> 03:01:01.075
I just want to echo everything that has been said. I think it's

03:01:01.575 --> 03:01:05.176
been well said. I woke up this

03:01:05.315 --> 03:01:08.938
morning, and, um, I'd had a conversation

03:01:09.438 --> 03:01:11.685
with a resident about this tax increase about a week ago.

03:01:12.726 --> 03:01:16.033
And I try to stay off social media,

03:01:16.049 --> 03:01:19.888
but sometimes I just can't resist and was punching

03:01:19.985 --> 03:01:24.111
out a response. And I thought, this just isn't an adequate way to

03:01:24.611 --> 03:01:28.618
communicate with residents through Facebook. You maybe get 3 paragraphs in, you don't

03:01:29.118 --> 03:01:32.054
quite communicate communicate what you want to. I know Colton here has,

03:01:32.262 --> 03:01:35.259
has been doing his best to be fair to everybody.

03:01:36.236 --> 03:01:39.666
Um, we thought about maybe doing a long-form podcast

03:01:39.698 --> 03:01:43.368
so you can get to the depth of the issue and really understand what

03:01:43.868 --> 03:01:47.310
is being presented and what the issue is. I've wrestled with this. I've worked through

03:01:47.810 --> 03:01:51.861
the numbers forward and backward as a financial planner. That's my vocation

03:01:52.361 --> 03:01:55.779
and what I do. And, um, it It is a challenge.

03:01:56.864 --> 03:02:00.183
It is a real challenge. It's something that I'm wrestling with.

03:02:02.647 --> 03:02:06.424
I may not agree with where the budget landed, but I do

03:02:06.924 --> 03:02:10.831
think we're understaffed. I've just got an email from a resident who

03:02:11.152 --> 03:02:14.961
sent pictures of city property around her house that's just

03:02:15.411 --> 03:02:19.390
6 inches. We're out of code on our

03:02:19.538 --> 03:02:23.251
own property. Yeah. Talk about code enforcement.

03:02:23.299 --> 03:02:26.396
We've got a lot of areas that have weeds, that the grass is dying.

03:02:26.653 --> 03:02:29.477
I live next to a park and it's not in great shape.

03:02:30.280 --> 03:02:33.746
And when you look at the numbers and see the staff, this last weekend

03:02:34.246 --> 03:02:37.549
was a great example of what I would call a stress test on one

03:02:37.597 --> 03:02:41.031
city, one part of the city. We had a fireworks show ready

03:02:41.531 --> 03:02:44.786
to go that went off really well. We had a

03:02:45.286 --> 03:02:48.140
fire department there to help support. On the other side of the city, city,

03:02:48.574 --> 03:02:52.235
poor Max Straw and his team trying to band-aid together some wells

03:02:52.267 --> 03:02:55.960
and get a message out, hey, we've got to, we've got to reserve

03:02:56.460 --> 03:03:00.375
what we're doing. And I was really impressed with all the residents between the fireworks,

03:03:00.696 --> 03:03:04.438
not being able to light those off on America's 250th anniversary.

03:03:04.935 --> 03:03:08.114
That's a hard ask. I think it was a really hard ask for our mayor

03:03:09.046 --> 03:03:12.514
to ask that of residents. I'm pretty sure this is a community that loves

03:03:12.562 --> 03:03:12.996
America.

03:03:16.608 --> 03:03:20.334
So our staff is amazing. They're tremendous. I've had just

03:03:20.590 --> 03:03:23.705
awesome interactions. I know they're stretched thin. They show up early.

03:03:23.978 --> 03:03:27.190
They stay late. In fact, we've got one, one of our

03:03:27.690 --> 03:03:31.204
staff members who was here last night trying to get a communication out that

03:03:31.704 --> 03:03:34.817
there was a leak and a flood. You probably saw it on Facebook. One of

03:03:35.317 --> 03:03:38.911
our family members, or one of our residents, their homes was being flooded

03:03:39.411 --> 03:03:43.338
this morning, and that communication was going out. He's here early this morning.

03:03:43.402 --> 03:03:46.609
He's going to be here late tonight. And I'm just really grateful for all that

03:03:47.109 --> 03:03:50.250
our staff does. I'm grateful for our community. My wife

03:03:50.378 --> 03:03:54.067
and I moved out here, I think, in 2005. And about

03:03:54.567 --> 03:03:57.146
in 2012, we had the opportunity to leave Eagle Mountain and move somewhere else,

03:03:57.274 --> 03:04:00.401
and it was just like a no for me. Like, this is, this is where

03:04:00.901 --> 03:04:04.266
we belong. We love, love the city. And I'm grateful to serve with everyone here.

03:04:04.395 --> 03:04:07.968
I'm grateful for for the staff for all that you do, and grateful for our

03:04:08.468 --> 03:04:10.734
community. We're going to make the right decision with this tax increase.

03:04:12.310 --> 03:04:15.913
On August 6th at— I think it's at 6 o'clock— we'll

03:04:16.269 --> 03:04:20.115
have a full-on conversation, and I really look forward

03:04:20.615 --> 03:04:23.961
to that. I hope people come and we can have a full dialogue so

03:04:24.461 --> 03:04:28.083
we can make the best possible decision for the city. So I

03:04:28.583 --> 03:04:31.040
look forward to that and just having more dialogue, and feel free to reach out

03:04:31.540 --> 03:04:34.876
to I appreciate, Bronwyn, your comments tonight. Thank you.

03:04:36.000 --> 03:04:39.470
Thanks, Mayor. Okay, thank you.

03:04:39.695 --> 03:04:43.549
Just, I would say, like Brett, ditto to everything. We really

03:04:44.049 --> 03:04:48.111
wrestled with the restrictions for the fireworks and worked a lot

03:04:48.611 --> 03:04:51.966
with our fire chief and our public safety staff, our admins, and the

03:04:52.466 --> 03:04:55.676
council to, to really— I wrestled with that

03:04:56.176 --> 03:04:59.643
for a long time, actually. Went back and forth with, can we find an area

03:04:59.835 --> 03:05:03.143
that's safe? And that just couldn't make sense of

03:05:03.643 --> 03:05:07.141
it. And finally had to pull the trigger and do that. But in the process,

03:05:07.157 --> 03:05:11.412
we were able to put a little bit of extra funding towards our city show.

03:05:11.878 --> 03:05:15.170
And it was absolutely epic. I did want to apologize last year.

03:05:15.250 --> 03:05:18.638
I always go alone and I drug you with me. So I didn't go alone

03:05:19.138 --> 03:05:22.074
this year. I abandoned you. So I was out of town and I didn't get

03:05:22.574 --> 03:05:24.771
to go to the show. I didn't get to go to the show, but next

03:05:25.271 --> 03:05:25.919
year, next year I'll go with you.

03:05:28.472 --> 03:05:31.638
We did the water restriction as well.

03:05:32.152 --> 03:05:37.165
You know, we've been just skating on thin ice there with— it's

03:05:37.214 --> 03:05:40.018
this fine line. I'm going to say what nobody wants to say. As soon as

03:05:40.066 --> 03:05:43.896
the city comes out and says, hey, we need to conserve water, everybody turns on

03:05:43.945 --> 03:05:47.389
their water and it has an opposite effect to hurry before it gets turned off.

03:05:48.107 --> 03:05:51.412
I didn't that this time, and we tried to really ease into that to make

03:05:51.912 --> 03:05:54.752
sure we didn't lose more water than we were producing.

03:05:55.651 --> 03:05:58.878
And through the weekend, I continued to get texts, hey, I turned

03:05:59.378 --> 03:06:02.106
my water off, how are we looking? And the best one was today,

03:06:02.876 --> 03:06:06.104
I still haven't done laundry for like 5 days, can I do my

03:06:06.604 --> 03:06:09.283
laundry? I'm like, we only asked you to not water your lawn.

03:06:10.102 --> 03:06:13.811
But people really went above and beyond, and I appreciate that,

03:06:13.891 --> 03:06:17.184
that they They felt the urgency and the need

03:06:17.684 --> 03:06:21.579
to help us get our wells back online. And our systems

03:06:21.643 --> 03:06:24.964
were good. Our staff— one other conversation I

03:06:25.464 --> 03:06:29.274
want to say earlier today. As council members, we sit up here and think—

03:06:29.921 --> 03:06:32.999
Ben and I and Brad and some of the staff have been talking about the

03:06:34.310 --> 03:06:37.602
92-ish projects that we're managing right now, like water tanks

03:06:37.651 --> 03:06:41.698
and roads and big, big projects. and how understaffed

03:06:42.198 --> 03:06:45.635
we are. Sewer treatment plant. Yeah, sewer treatment plant, which is one of the biggest.

03:06:46.824 --> 03:06:49.861
And then as council members, we're up here, why hasn't this happened? Why hasn't that

03:06:50.361 --> 03:06:53.830
happened? And we allocate money in the budget and expect things to happen overnight.

03:06:54.617 --> 03:06:57.863
And it's funny because I, you know, we get pushback sometimes from staff.

03:06:58.554 --> 03:07:01.751
And I said, you know, the funny thing is our staff is so good that

03:07:01.784 --> 03:07:05.158
we keep throwing things on their plate and they look at us like, there's no

03:07:05.222 --> 03:07:08.289
way we can do this. But then they just smile and say, we'll give it

03:07:08.789 --> 03:07:12.512
our best shot. And they go out and get it done and continue to overperform

03:07:12.528 --> 03:07:15.900
when we continue to pile stuff on them. So, you know,

03:07:15.916 --> 03:07:18.645
guys that worked through the night last night and they're still here today,

03:07:19.432 --> 03:07:22.884
it's the dedication. I mean, I don't think

03:07:23.384 --> 03:07:25.019
most of us realize, but I don't think most of us would do it.

03:07:25.613 --> 03:07:28.905
And they continue to come. So we really do have a great staff.

03:07:29.065 --> 03:07:32.919
And you can do your laundry. So everybody— heard that from

03:07:32.935 --> 03:07:34.717
me, go ahead and wash your clothes.

03:07:36.496 --> 03:07:39.741
And shower. And shower. But continue to do your best on your, on your

03:07:39.790 --> 03:07:43.325
grass. I mean, we— I think we all overwater

03:07:43.825 --> 03:07:47.652
our grass. It doesn't have to be that dark, dark green. Just keep it alive,

03:07:47.764 --> 03:07:51.453
I think, this year is the goal. So conserve where we can. That would

03:07:51.953 --> 03:07:55.635
be my, my ask. And we

03:07:56.135 --> 03:07:59.057
do appreciate you guys all supporting and all the events that we do and that

03:07:59.089 --> 03:08:03.259
we can come together and be

03:08:03.323 --> 03:08:07.256
a community. I said this last time that what makes us a community is

03:08:07.756 --> 03:08:10.084
the fact that we still wave at each other and say hi at the grocery

03:08:10.584 --> 03:08:14.447
store and we're still personable. We haven't

03:08:15.319 --> 03:08:18.751
got into our little boxes and, you know, we treat

03:08:19.251 --> 03:08:22.686
each other with respect. So appreciate everything that was said. And with that,

03:08:23.107 --> 03:08:26.332
I think we better get some work done. Thank you. We're going to move on

03:08:26.832 --> 03:08:29.930
to our consent agenda. I do have a question. I know item 15A and

03:08:29.962 --> 03:08:33.442
15D, there's questions. If that motion looks like

03:08:33.942 --> 03:08:37.605
it's going to muddy up the other consent motion,

