you guys are trying to approve they had to get bonds done because we were

03:23:38.120 --> 03:23:40.380
in— that was one of our meetings, the stuff. Will you please put the roads

03:23:40.880 --> 03:23:43.460
in? You have bonds. Will you please? Well, they got some legal problems there.

03:23:43.960 --> 03:23:46.750
I mean, is it— it's just like it just kept coming up and coming up.

03:23:47.340 --> 03:23:50.560
I need to correct myself. You are outside of the town center on there.

03:23:51.060 --> 03:23:53.420
You're adjacent. So, okay, I want to make sure I correct that. I'm sorry.

03:23:53.920 --> 03:23:55.503
That's why we keep talking about it going, I didn't know we were the town

03:23:56.003 --> 03:23:59.292
center concept. So— I, I think

03:24:00.159 --> 03:24:03.692
I'll just say this. What we contemplated back then, it's grown well beyond

03:24:04.192 --> 03:24:07.385
that. You're, you're going to be part of that as, as the neighbor to the

03:24:07.885 --> 03:24:11.335
west, because we never anticipated having big users that

03:24:11.835 --> 03:24:15.670
are there. That's going to change that area. And so what was originally contemplated

03:24:15.750 --> 03:24:18.881
is going to change. But I think that there's a route for you still

03:24:19.381 --> 03:24:22.944
to have a very profitable project there, just not in

03:24:22.960 --> 03:24:26.267
the— in my opinion, not in the contemplation that

03:24:26.767 --> 03:24:31.257
it sits at right now. And we don't want to hurt anybody,

03:24:31.692 --> 03:24:36.345
but we want cohesiveness, transition, and we want the

03:24:36.845 --> 03:24:40.795
downtown to feel like we did it on purpose. So I guess

03:24:41.295 --> 03:24:44.717
I have not— is the downtown up by a parcel? Because we have the road

03:24:45.217 --> 03:24:48.055
on the one side. They're adjacent. We have Ivory on the other, and we have—

03:24:48.408 --> 03:24:51.697
but I didn't think— I thought your town center was a little bit— that's why

03:24:52.197 --> 03:24:54.039
when you're talking about the town center, I'm going, well, to me it's— When we

03:24:54.539 --> 03:24:56.702
say downtown, we're not just talking about the piece of land that we own.

03:24:56.927 --> 03:25:00.264
It's the area around it. Oh, I understand. Yes, you're part of that.

03:25:00.842 --> 03:25:05.254
Ivory to the north is for sure. Their Overland project,

03:25:05.334 --> 03:25:08.880
they're going to be part of that. Our hope is— I mean, they've got a

03:25:09.200 --> 03:25:12.623
development agreement that we don't have the ability to alter.

03:25:12.979 --> 03:25:16.743
Our hope is that they will be good neighbors and help with

03:25:17.243 --> 03:25:20.086
some of those architectural designs so it looks like we all did this on purpose.

03:25:21.637 --> 03:25:24.929
But where you're asking for something that we have the ability to come back and

03:25:24.961 --> 03:25:27.753
negotiate, I think that that's— again,

03:25:28.269 --> 03:25:31.052
I want to give everybody else a chance to talk. It's not my show here.

03:25:31.272 --> 03:25:32.641
This is just where I stand.

03:25:34.380 --> 03:25:36.797
Thank you.

03:25:38.261 --> 03:25:41.697
So I think part of the challenge that sort of gets thrown at

03:25:42.197 --> 03:25:46.016
the City Council is the agenda gets posted, got posted late on Thursday,

03:25:46.032 --> 03:25:49.067
July 2nd. July 3rd, a lot of family stuff.

03:25:49.163 --> 03:25:52.535
July 4th was a lot of family stuff. Then we're hit with Sunday,

03:25:52.599 --> 03:25:56.212
Monday, and we had over 400 pages to read covering like numerous different

03:25:56.712 --> 03:26:01.447
topics. And so I think fortunately for this specific one, your agreement

03:26:01.947 --> 03:26:05.279
has— It's a lot. Till July 24th, I think the current one.

03:26:05.859 --> 03:26:09.392
So if it were to be tabled per your suggestion, that would

03:26:09.425 --> 03:26:12.880
give at least a— it would be able to resurface in 2 weeks still

03:26:12.897 --> 03:26:16.282
before that date, and between now and then we can have more conversations

03:26:16.782 --> 03:26:20.518
about that. And so anyway, I think just trying

03:26:21.018 --> 03:26:23.014
to grasp, put our minds around it, at least I'm going to say put my

03:26:23.514 --> 03:26:26.540
mind around it given everything else we're contemplating at the same time. I think it

03:26:27.040 --> 03:26:30.475
would be healthy to see sit down and have

03:26:30.507 --> 03:26:33.957
some meetings and conversation about what is sort of happening

03:26:34.457 --> 03:26:37.374
in the vicinity and see if there needs to be any modifications or changes.

03:26:37.791 --> 03:26:41.497
I will say I like the fact that there's good segmentation within the development.

03:26:41.802 --> 03:26:45.444
I like the fact that there's some bigger lots in there.

03:26:45.573 --> 03:26:49.231
And I think that's, you know, there's some healthy, I think there's some healthy way

03:26:49.263 --> 03:26:51.846
to the, it was, you know, there's obviously a lot of thought I think that

03:26:52.346 --> 03:26:56.061
went into the design of that. But and I think before,

03:26:56.191 --> 03:26:59.660
I mean, I'm not ready to just put an agreement out

03:27:00.160 --> 03:27:03.372
to 2033 because I agree with Council Member Wood in that I hope that city

03:27:03.872 --> 03:27:07.536
councils take the the sort of the procedure

03:27:08.036 --> 03:27:11.459
that we're only approving stuff that on the residential side that can be acted

03:27:12.219 --> 03:27:15.910
basically now instead of down the road. And so like my initial contemplation

03:27:15.943 --> 03:27:17.837
when I saw this, I'm like, well, if we can't act on it now,

03:27:18.598 --> 03:27:21.654
then let's let it expire and then let's. reevaluate it once

03:27:22.154 --> 03:27:25.678
it's time to be developed. That being said,

03:27:25.694 --> 03:27:28.296
you've put a lot of effort into it, and that's why I think it merits

03:27:28.796 --> 03:27:31.369
not just saying, well, let's just let it expire and let's reconsider. I think,

03:27:32.017 --> 03:27:35.496
in my mind, it merits a tabling, some conversation between now

03:27:35.996 --> 03:27:37.875
and the next 2 weeks, putting it back on the agenda 2 weeks from now,

03:27:38.183 --> 03:27:41.508
and going from there. And that's where I'm standing as

03:27:41.556 --> 03:27:45.115
well at that. I've heard at

03:27:45.615 --> 03:27:48.292
least 2 people say table. I know we're not going to work it out tonight.

03:27:48.759 --> 03:27:52.046
Is that the direction we're going to head, or is it— we're not going

03:27:52.078 --> 03:27:55.850
to be able to negotiate from the dais is my point. So I'm

03:27:56.350 --> 03:28:00.634
looking for either opposition to that or support

03:28:01.134 --> 03:28:04.644
for that from the other 3 of you. And let's get this

03:28:09.311 --> 03:28:13.261
Let me ask this question. Is there anybody who's in favor of putting this Proposal

03:28:13.761 --> 03:28:16.716
forward. As written? As written.

03:28:21.560 --> 03:28:25.804
No, I mean, I frankly, I— this

03:28:25.853 --> 03:28:28.774
one, this one, I'm conflicted on this one, Curt. I'm just going to tell you

03:28:29.274 --> 03:28:32.469
straight up. We worked really hard to get it to this place.

03:28:32.794 --> 03:28:35.470
I mean, I was here when we went through this. You remember.

03:28:38.202 --> 03:28:44.482
And I'm not overly disappointed

03:28:44.982 --> 03:28:47.050
in the lot sizes that we got in there. I mean, the smallest lots are

03:28:47.550 --> 03:28:50.696
8,000 square feet. So, but what I am disappointed

03:28:51.196 --> 03:28:54.584
about is that it's basically set inactive and you've tried to share

03:28:55.084 --> 03:28:57.480
the reasons why. And I think some of them are,

03:28:59.826 --> 03:29:02.959
have at least the ability for us to consider that,

03:29:02.975 --> 03:29:05.947
right, as an option. But I also think there's probably some work that could have

03:29:05.979 --> 03:29:09.607
been done that hasn't been done, and maybe I'm off base there.

03:29:09.912 --> 03:29:13.123
I'm— but that's kind of my sense. I mean, it's like

03:29:13.623 --> 03:29:16.880
the airport road, just working with Citlin, knowing that that's going to happen. That hasn't

03:29:17.380 --> 03:29:20.829
changed. That alignment hasn't changed. There's nothing different about that. And if you haven't already

03:29:20.909 --> 03:29:24.120
procured that with them, that just tells me that you haven't

03:29:24.184 --> 03:29:28.824
pursued that with the— perhaps with the energy

03:29:28.856 --> 03:29:32.289
and effort that I would hope you would. That very

03:29:32.789 --> 03:29:36.218
specifically. Maybe there's some adjustments up on the north end that you're dealing with and

03:29:36.250 --> 03:29:39.855
contemplating, but that airport road alignment, I don't know why we're still talking about you

03:29:39.871 --> 03:29:42.257
have to work with Sitla to figure that out. That should be figured out in

03:29:42.757 --> 03:29:46.057
my mind. So that's where I'm

03:29:46.089 --> 03:29:49.383
doing this, going, is it better just to let it expire

03:29:49.883 --> 03:29:53.191
and let's just, let's have another bite at the apple, or do we, do I

03:29:53.320 --> 03:29:57.276
listen to what my council members are suggesting and We table

03:29:57.776 --> 03:30:01.143
it, we have some negotiation, we have some discussion, and where does that take

03:30:01.643 --> 03:30:05.509
us? That's really my question, because I'd like to see you get going.

03:30:05.942 --> 03:30:09.280
Well, and as you talk about the Sitland and the bypass road, the bypass road

03:30:09.312 --> 03:30:13.068
goes across to Aviator, so that's why I think they're bringing it

03:30:13.568 --> 03:30:17.401
as part of that, the Aviator intersection. That's my— and

03:30:17.901 --> 03:30:20.290
so I think the staff's been working with it, you know, and that's why we've

03:30:20.790 --> 03:30:23.500
been with— I, I read like to be We were open, probably, you know,

03:30:24.000 --> 03:30:27.770
we have talked. I sat down with Brad several times or with Ivory.

03:30:27.920 --> 03:30:30.200
And yeah, he'd probably be agreeable. I mean, I think, I don't know if you

03:30:30.700 --> 03:30:34.020
noticed, they have a church site that's there right on Aviator Avenue that they're going

03:30:34.520 --> 03:30:36.840
to have to start getting some access. So we, when you ask, you know,

03:30:37.340 --> 03:30:39.480
we've kind of thought that sometime they're going to have to get some access there

03:30:39.980 --> 03:30:43.620
to kind of put that road in, you know. And so, you know, that's closed.

03:30:44.120 --> 03:30:47.060
It's in the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints' name.

03:30:47.560 --> 03:30:50.580
Yes. we've been watching, you know, all that going on that we're going,

03:30:50.590 --> 03:30:54.110
okay guys, why aren't you finishing the road? You just sold property here.

03:30:54.610 --> 03:30:56.810
What are you doing? You know, I mean, so we're watching. We've been talking with

03:30:57.310 --> 03:31:00.290
them about it. So maybe I would ask this question, Mayor, if there are any

03:31:00.790 --> 03:31:04.370
other questions that council members have of the applicant. And if not, then let's,

03:31:04.870 --> 03:31:08.230
let's, let's ask him to, to take a seat. Let's have a discussion as

03:31:08.730 --> 03:31:10.530
a council. And if there's 2 council members that want to make a motion to

03:31:11.030 --> 03:31:14.100
table, then let's entertain that. That's fair with me.

03:31:14.763 --> 03:31:17.334
If you want, we'll go ahead and have a discussion and let's see where this

03:31:17.834 --> 03:31:21.701
goes. They've heard your ask and I appreciate that. We'll see

03:31:21.765 --> 03:31:25.548
what we can come up with. Thank you.

03:31:26.207 --> 03:31:29.116
I think these two have definitely— No, I don't think that I've said we're going

03:31:29.616 --> 03:31:32.534
to table. I mean, I'm going to tell you, we're talking now. Okay.

03:31:35.060 --> 03:31:38.558
I'm— in my mind, I'm trying to work out what did

03:31:39.058 --> 03:31:42.983
the applicant think think today was going to be. We've talked about all these scenarios

03:31:43.032 --> 03:31:46.389
about what Citrus is going to do, what they're not going to do,

03:31:46.470 --> 03:31:49.379
what Ivory is going to do, what they're not going to do. You know,

03:31:49.460 --> 03:31:52.713
Pulte Homes wasn't able to do their part. You know,

03:31:53.360 --> 03:31:55.999
D.R. Horton pulled out. I mean, we've gone through all those scenarios.

03:31:58.289 --> 03:32:01.675
What is the idea to come in and ask for an extension?

03:32:02.194 --> 03:32:02.583
For what?

03:32:05.710 --> 03:32:09.195
We've literally seen no movement on this property. To your

03:32:09.695 --> 03:32:13.535
point, Brett, you make a good point. We haven't seen anything negotiating the parts

03:32:14.035 --> 03:32:17.145
that can be negotiated to the north that have nothing to do with the south.

03:32:19.312 --> 03:32:23.118
I find myself at a crossroads here of if we were to table it,

03:32:23.360 --> 03:32:26.950
to what end? What is it that we can negotiate

03:32:26.998 --> 03:32:30.496
in a 2-week time period that would be

03:32:30.996 --> 03:32:34.107
of any value to make this project move forward, because I don't believe that he

03:32:34.607 --> 03:32:38.144
has the ability to move forward. If we give

03:32:38.644 --> 03:32:41.984
the extension, we're going to be back here again giving another extension because it's

03:32:42.484 --> 03:32:45.967
not ready. And so I, I'm struggling with

03:32:46.016 --> 03:32:49.812
this because I, I think all of you know how much I

03:32:50.312 --> 03:32:53.595
feel about getting to the project.

03:32:54.500 --> 03:32:58.069
I mean, I was pretty instrumental in writing the MDH code

03:32:58.327 --> 03:33:01.927
for that very reason, is to make sure that we no longer became

03:33:02.992 --> 03:33:06.254
a city that was full of land brokers, that we

03:33:06.625 --> 03:33:10.153
got too busy. So I'm not going to make the motion

03:33:10.202 --> 03:33:14.167
for a table. I'm probably in the

03:33:14.667 --> 03:33:15.510
place of let's start over.

03:33:18.506 --> 03:33:21.640
When you say let's start over— Just let it expire. Well, yeah, but I

03:33:22.140 --> 03:33:25.347
was just saying, Even if it does expire, all of

03:33:25.861 --> 03:33:29.024
the conceptual engineering work's done. So even if it's expired

03:33:29.313 --> 03:33:32.781
and it's over per se, it's not hard to start back over.

03:33:32.926 --> 03:33:36.442
Well, yeah, they're not ready to go though. I think that's evident. And when

03:33:36.474 --> 03:33:39.332
they're ready to go, they can come back in, do another application for it,

03:33:39.348 --> 03:33:42.624
and we can get started with the new MDA code. It's going to be

03:33:43.124 --> 03:33:46.397
far more restrictive than the one they had. So just for the

03:33:46.413 --> 03:33:49.790
record, the agreement doesn't expire until 2029. They would simply be in breach.

03:33:52.630 --> 03:33:56.310
So those are 2 different things. Why don't you give us further explanation

03:33:56.810 --> 03:33:59.000
of what in breach means then? It means they'd be in breach of the agreement.

03:33:59.230 --> 03:34:03.010
We'd have, you know, potential remedies under the agreement, including, you know,

03:34:03.510 --> 03:34:06.750
a lawsuit for breach of contract. We could withhold municipal

03:34:07.250 --> 03:34:10.330
approvals related to the project. Since they're not seeking any, I don't think that's really

03:34:10.830 --> 03:34:14.230
an issue at this point. And so we

03:34:14.730 --> 03:34:18.043
could seek kind of traditional remedies in contract law. It's not—

03:34:18.543 --> 03:34:21.690
the agreement would still be in effect, but we wouldn't have to live up to

03:34:22.190 --> 03:34:25.359
our end for the time being. Now, they could potentially cure the breach at some

03:34:25.392 --> 03:34:29.057
point in the future, but that's kind of where we'd be at. The agreement

03:34:29.557 --> 03:34:32.402
wouldn't actually be expired. Let them go into breach then. Yeah,

03:34:33.780 --> 03:34:35.768
there's nothing that we need to solve for them then.

03:34:39.880 --> 03:34:43.101
Mayor, will you accept a motion? I will,

03:34:43.117 --> 03:34:45.429
as long as those who haven't spoken have said that they're good.

03:34:45.978 --> 03:34:50.052
Did you want to weigh in? I think I felt like—

03:34:50.133 --> 03:34:53.144
I felt like I had a chance during work session to say what I thought,

03:34:53.161 --> 03:34:55.447
so— Okay, then, then I'm good.

03:34:58.094 --> 03:35:01.557
You'll accept a motion? Mayor, I move that the City Council deny

03:35:02.057 --> 03:35:05.325
a resolution of Eagle Mountain City, Utah approving the 2nd amendment to the Eagle Crest

03:35:05.406 --> 03:35:08.926
East Master Development Agreement. Second. Okay, we have a motion

03:35:08.959 --> 03:35:12.644
by Councilmember Wood and a second by Councilmember Whiting. Any questions?

03:35:12.692 --> 03:35:15.732
That's pretty straightforward, so we'll start the vote with, uh, Councilmember Wood.

03:35:16.980 --> 03:35:19.996
Yes. Councilmember Huish. Yes. Councilmember Clark.

03:35:20.693 --> 03:35:22.168
Yes. Councilmember Wright.

03:35:23.778 --> 03:35:26.372
I'll come back to you. Councilmember Whiting. Yes.

03:35:27.854 --> 03:35:28.950
Yes. Okay.

03:35:32.658 --> 03:35:36.286
All right. We are moving

03:35:36.366 --> 03:35:40.011
on to item 16A, an ordinance of Eagle Mountain City,

03:35:40.043 --> 03:35:43.450
Utah, enacting Chapter 17.64 of the Eagle

03:35:43.950 --> 03:35:48.035
Mountain Municipal Code, establishing an alternate— alternative energy overlay

03:35:48.535 --> 03:35:52.206
zone. And this Evan Barrett, he's been back in Washington,

03:35:52.642 --> 03:35:56.199
and I know you met with a lot of people that influenced

03:35:56.699 --> 03:35:59.331
this, so you have a lot of good information. Um,

03:36:01.816 --> 03:36:05.087
I know it's going to be a good discussion, so we'll turn

03:36:05.587 --> 03:36:09.388
it over. Great. Then you'd

03:36:09.888 --> 03:36:12.622
get an application to do a rezoning.

03:36:14.933 --> 03:36:18.619
There's so many public hearings between now and then.

03:36:18.911 --> 03:36:33.912
This, uh, work— Maybe.

03:36:34.217 --> 03:36:36.959
We used to call these gremlins. I don't know if you have a new name.

03:36:37.809 --> 03:36:41.433
Everything's black up here, so Carson maybe

03:36:41.498 --> 03:36:44.481
can help us. I was joking that you were IT before me.

03:36:44.865 --> 03:36:47.929
Yeah. Let's try that

03:36:48.429 --> 03:36:49.035
and see if that works.

03:36:52.360 --> 03:37:00.289
Does that

03:37:00.789 --> 03:37:00.852
show up now?

03:37:10.149 --> 03:37:13.220
I'm just wondering, I was kind of giving it half a second.

03:37:14.012 --> 03:37:17.067
I'm looking forward to see what doesn't have a presentation that maybe

03:37:17.083 --> 03:37:18.969
we could just be talking about why they're Working on this.

03:37:21.102 --> 03:37:24.784
Is 16C the accessory recreation building?

03:37:24.881 --> 03:37:28.467
Could we maybe— could we jump to that?

03:37:28.531 --> 03:37:31.815
Why? So you guys keep working, Evan, and I'm going

03:37:32.315 --> 03:37:35.485
to jump to 16C, an ordinance of Eagle Mountain City,

03:37:35.663 --> 03:37:41.292
Utah, amending the Eagle Mountain Municipal Code 1775.052

03:37:41.792 --> 03:37:45.581
regarding accessory recreation buildings. This is Steven,

03:37:45.613 --> 03:37:48.746
as far as questions. There's no presentation per se,

03:37:49.405 --> 03:37:52.490
or yes? Just the code.

03:37:52.908 --> 03:37:55.439
Okay. And we have it here in the packet, I believe.

03:37:56.316 --> 03:38:00.051
So let's start off with questions

03:38:00.551 --> 03:38:02.633
if we can go there, and then we'll bring Steven up if we need to.

03:38:03.500 --> 03:38:06.762
Steven, it's not going to help because we can't

03:38:07.262 --> 03:38:11.290
get on the screen, but maybe jump on that mic over there where where

03:38:11.355 --> 03:38:14.245
Brandon was if you want, and we'll work on this.

03:38:16.067 --> 03:38:18.296
So I'll open it up for discussion or questions.

03:38:20.929 --> 03:38:24.535
I'm just grateful that there was some creativity

03:38:24.792 --> 03:38:29.300
in this solution and

03:38:29.799 --> 03:38:33.032
that the staff brought this forward before us. So I don't really have any questions

03:38:33.048 --> 03:38:36.799
on this one. Okay, we do have the applicant here as well.

03:38:37.268 --> 03:38:40.418
I couldn't quite see, but just in case there's questions for the applicant, right?

03:38:42.664 --> 03:38:46.095
No questions from Rich, Zach. Okay.

03:38:50.622 --> 03:38:53.503
Oh, there's a public hearing on this one. I was just making sure that I

03:38:54.003 --> 03:38:57.597
covered everything. So this is a public hearing, and we'll go ahead

03:38:57.662 --> 03:39:00.363
to that point now. So at 8:28, I will open the public hearing.

03:39:01.146 --> 03:39:04.480
And ask, I know you're busy over there, but is there anyone that

03:39:04.980 --> 03:39:08.343
came to speak on this? No. Okay, I didn't mean to distract you. No,

03:39:08.391 --> 03:39:12.062
no one. Is there anyone in the audience that did not get a chance

03:39:12.562 --> 03:39:15.428
to sign up for this item that would like to speak?

03:39:16.566 --> 03:39:20.717
Seeing no one, I will close the public hearing on the

03:39:21.217 --> 03:39:24.917
16C and bring it back to the council. If there's no questions, I will entertain

03:39:24.965 --> 03:39:27.641
a motion. I just wanted to make a comment. Thank you,

03:39:27.994 --> 03:39:31.759
first of all, for engaging in this process. Appreciate the work

03:39:32.259 --> 03:39:35.488
that you did. And this was Steven that did this one, is that correct?

03:39:36.133 --> 03:39:39.375
Yeah. Steven, it's well-written code. I don't know where— I can't see you.

03:39:39.520 --> 03:39:43.015
I don't know where you are. There you are. I think you did a

03:39:43.048 --> 03:39:47.034
great job. It's concise, it's to the point. I think it addresses the

03:39:47.534 --> 03:39:50.952
concerns, at least that I had, and creates a path

03:39:51.065 --> 03:39:54.867
for any other religious organization that

03:39:55.367 --> 03:39:58.438
is looking to do a culture hall, that we've put a standard in place,

03:39:59.193 --> 03:40:02.084
uh, that will enable them to have the same rights. And I, I am a

03:40:02.584 --> 03:40:05.964
fan of consistency so that everybody, uh, can enjoy the same, uh,

03:40:06.109 --> 03:40:09.844
benefits. So I'm happy with it.

03:40:10.653 --> 03:40:12.399
Motion? Yeah, make a motion.

03:40:16.653 --> 03:40:20.199
Yeah, which one is it? 16D. Mayor, I move that

03:40:20.699 --> 03:40:22.691
the city council approve an Sorry, 16C.

03:40:23.597 --> 03:40:26.995
16C. Yeah, there we go. I move that the

03:40:27.495 --> 03:40:31.509
city council approve an ordinance of Eagle Mountain City, Utah, amending the Eagle Mountain Municipal

03:40:32.009 --> 03:40:35.590
Code 17.75.052 regarding

03:40:35.703 --> 03:40:38.174
accessory recreation buildings. Second.

03:40:40.774 --> 03:40:43.751
Okay, we have a motion by Council Member Wood and a second by Council Member

03:40:43.784 --> 03:40:47.065
White, and we'll start the vote with Council Member Huish. Yes. Council Member Clark.

03:40:47.282 --> 03:40:50.823
Yes. Councilmember Wright? Yes. Councilmember Whiting? Yes. Councilmember Wood?

03:40:51.324 --> 03:40:55.576
Yes. Okay, that passes. All right, it looks like we're going to go backwards

03:40:55.689 --> 03:40:57.722
to 16A, and Evan, you're up.

03:40:58.609 --> 03:41:01.706
Okay, apologies for the technical difficulties.

03:41:02.787 --> 03:41:05.755
I think we're good to go now. So I appreciate the opportunity, Mayor and Council,

03:41:07.942 --> 03:41:11.682
to bring to you the alternative energy overlay zone. It's been through a few

03:41:12.182 --> 03:41:15.665
different iterations, And as mentioned in kind

03:41:16.165 --> 03:41:20.126
of the introduction that the mayor provided, a lot of people have seen it and

03:41:20.626 --> 03:41:24.460
it's been seen by a lot of great experts as well. So just

03:41:24.960 --> 03:41:28.118
wanted to give a little bit of an intro to this, just some things that

03:41:28.618 --> 03:41:31.368
we haven't really talked and explicitly said before,

03:41:33.288 --> 03:41:36.767
and mostly for the purposes of the public here, for our

03:41:37.267 --> 03:41:40.543
general approach to energy. I just want to make mention

03:41:40.575 --> 03:41:44.182
that we are looking at this alternative energy overlay

03:41:44.888 --> 03:41:48.671
zone primarily for the purposes of helping to provide energy

03:41:49.171 --> 03:41:52.406
generation opportunities for our commercial and industrial users.

03:41:53.080 --> 03:41:56.991
We treat, as we look at a lot of this, the residential

03:41:57.491 --> 03:42:01.095
and the commercial industrial uses as separate, where obviously our

03:42:01.595 --> 03:42:04.738
residential uses we want to protect and safeguard and make sure those are

03:42:05.238 --> 03:42:08.036
never impacted by anything that is coming to the city.

03:42:10.316 --> 03:42:14.054
We're not looking to take away from any residential energy supply

03:42:14.360 --> 03:42:17.599
or stability. That is of the utmost importance for us.

03:42:19.466 --> 03:42:23.388
And that advancing energy development in the city as a priority

03:42:23.661 --> 03:42:27.004
is for the specific purposes of attracting more jobs,

03:42:27.599 --> 03:42:31.610
more tax revenue, broadening our tax space and

03:42:31.690 --> 03:42:34.677
overall elevating the quality of life for Eagle Mountain residents.

03:42:35.865 --> 03:42:39.703
And so anything that would lead towards any sort of negative consequences,

03:42:39.735 --> 03:42:43.476
whether it be increased rates or increased

03:42:43.893 --> 03:42:47.506
risk of anything, we're obviously very opposed to seeing that happen.

03:42:47.554 --> 03:42:51.247
So we've crafted this overlay zone

03:42:51.536 --> 03:42:55.470
and worked with experts and worked with industry with that understanding in mind

03:42:55.970 --> 03:42:59.113
that we are looking at this as an opportunity and not meant to be anything

03:42:59.177 --> 03:43:02.128
that detracts from anything that we hold dear in Eagle Mountain.

03:43:04.154 --> 03:43:07.374
Really, a lot of this comes back to economic development for our

03:43:07.874 --> 03:43:11.287
city. For us, having reliable energy is

03:43:11.399 --> 03:43:14.637
not just a nice thing to have. It's something that

03:43:15.137 --> 03:43:17.733
we obviously very much need in a residential setting,

03:43:18.330 --> 03:43:22.173
but it's also extremely important in a commercial and industrial setting. And nowadays,

03:43:22.671 --> 03:43:26.799
more than ever, having abundant energy is

03:43:27.299 --> 03:43:30.879
a big determinant in economic development growth. It's a big

03:43:30.943 --> 03:43:34.316
determinant in site selection for the large companies that we hope

03:43:34.816 --> 03:43:38.476
to bring, really any size company, small or large nowadays,

03:43:39.793 --> 03:43:43.456
that could bring quality jobs for our residents and

03:43:43.488 --> 03:43:47.748
that broader tax base. As a whole, The state

03:43:48.248 --> 03:43:51.750
of Utah is working through energy, but it is underdeveloping.

03:43:51.863 --> 03:43:55.139
It is not developing quickly enough, and there's

03:43:55.364 --> 03:43:58.855
no guarantees at this point that Eagle Mountain will have enough energy

03:43:59.032 --> 03:44:02.266
for its economic development purposes, just relying on what's

03:44:02.766 --> 03:44:04.319
already underway across the state.

03:44:06.466 --> 03:44:09.702
There's certainly been questions, I think, among residents and

03:44:10.202 --> 03:44:13.674
others about data centers and want to talk about that really quick

03:44:14.259 --> 03:44:17.121
and then how we're looking at this broader and philosophically.

03:44:19.003 --> 03:44:22.141
There is some benefit, you know, of course, of having more energy

03:44:22.238 --> 03:44:25.893
abundance to be able to serve the data centers that will be built

03:44:26.393 --> 03:44:29.903
in Eagle Mountain to help those continue to build out. Eagle Mountain

03:44:30.178 --> 03:44:33.945
years ago looked at bringing data centers to our city

03:44:34.705 --> 03:44:37.901
because we saw there was a lot of advantages if brought in right and if

03:44:38.401 --> 03:44:40.843
we're worked with them in a proper partnership and collaborative fashion,

03:44:42.240 --> 03:44:46.110
that they can be great community assets that bring significant

03:44:46.610 --> 03:44:50.493
property tax and municipal energy tax revenue, and they bring high-quality jobs.

03:44:50.847 --> 03:44:54.235
But they're also very low impact to our community in multiple respects,

03:44:55.262 --> 03:44:59.180
and especially in a very low-cost service. I don't know that most

03:44:59.680 --> 03:45:02.825
people realize that a lot of the development that Eagle

03:45:03.325 --> 03:45:06.855
Mountain has seen right Right now, especially in residential development, it costs more to service

03:45:06.903 --> 03:45:10.147
those homes than it does in property tax we actually collect from

03:45:10.179 --> 03:45:13.615
them. Data centers are the opposite of that. It takes very

03:45:13.631 --> 03:45:17.179
little to service them and we get a significant amount of revenue that

03:45:17.679 --> 03:45:22.591
comes out of those. We've also seen with the current data centers that are

03:45:22.639 --> 03:45:26.589
built and are being built, there are other multiple positive outcomes

03:45:27.089 --> 03:45:30.699
that have been coming from them, from the infrastructure that's come through

03:45:30.892 --> 03:45:34.408
from the temporary jobs, temporary and permanent jobs, other commercial growth,

03:45:34.761 --> 03:45:38.856
the direct community giving, and things like that. And so

03:45:39.241 --> 03:45:42.677
at this point, you know, there is a benefit in seeing those

03:45:42.709 --> 03:45:45.969
continue to develop. However, there is a much

03:45:46.242 --> 03:45:50.015
bigger picture than this when it comes to energy. It's not all

03:45:50.515 --> 03:45:54.511
about the data centers. It is about economic development as a whole. Whether you're

03:45:55.011 --> 03:45:58.927
being factories or research centers or whatever they may

03:45:59.427 --> 03:46:03.053
be, they all need energy. And right now in the state of Utah,

03:46:03.984 --> 03:46:07.565
we're not sure that we are going to have enough to be able to

03:46:07.581 --> 03:46:11.065
help see Eagle Mountain grow to what it can really be and have the

03:46:11.565 --> 03:46:14.903
jobs here locally to help turn traffic around and help make sure

03:46:15.403 --> 03:46:18.981
that, you know, everyone has a great quality job they can go

03:46:19.481 --> 03:46:23.462
to. Thank you. Some questions

03:46:23.478 --> 03:46:26.916
we also see is why not just wait and why Eagle Mountain?

03:46:27.895 --> 03:46:31.734
Right now this is really, in my mind, it comes down to one primary

03:46:31.831 --> 03:46:35.605
answer and that is outcome control. So if we're not proactive

03:46:36.105 --> 03:46:39.765
and support power generation growth right now, commercial industrial development is

03:46:39.878 --> 03:46:43.315
much slower. Projects developing outside of

03:46:43.815 --> 03:46:47.941
Eagle Mountain city boundaries may not provide adequate

03:46:48.441 --> 03:46:49.935
power to Eagle Mountain for our projects, and we want to make sure that we

03:46:50.435 --> 03:46:53.940
do have that available to us. And we also potentially lose

03:46:54.037 --> 03:46:56.278
out on opportunities for tax revenues,

03:46:57.035 --> 03:46:58.728
jobs, and additional economic development.

03:47:01.119 --> 03:47:04.436
I want to talk a little bit too about what this alternative energy overlay zone

03:47:04.484 --> 03:47:08.236
is meant to do. It's not meant to just focus on one particular energy

03:47:08.268 --> 03:47:12.599
type. Our valley can support multiple types of power generation,

03:47:12.648 --> 03:47:16.450
whether it be solar, geothermal, nuclear, or natural gas.

03:47:18.780 --> 03:47:22.136
When we look at power generation types, there's a lot of different technologies within these

03:47:22.169 --> 03:47:25.891
categories. And as we've looked at putting

03:47:25.907 --> 03:47:29.920
this energy overlay zone together, water was a really big consideration

03:47:30.435 --> 03:47:33.870
as far as how we frame this overlay zone to make sure we are carefully

03:47:34.887 --> 03:47:39.116
narrowing down what some

03:47:39.616 --> 03:47:41.407
sorts of power generation uses we want to see in this valley. There's a lot

03:47:41.907 --> 03:47:46.307
of different types of solar, geothermal, nuclear, and natural gas, but there are several

03:47:46.387 --> 03:47:50.066
ones that are extremely safe and advanced and use very

03:47:50.082 --> 03:47:51.742
little water, and so that has been the priority.

03:47:54.315 --> 03:47:57.581
If I can get my

03:47:57.630 --> 03:48:01.329
thing to advance. So just a quick

03:48:01.829 --> 03:48:06.413
history, too, as far as how we've gotten to where we are. In early 2025.

03:48:06.526 --> 03:48:10.049
We originally approached this by looking to amend the RTI

03:48:10.130 --> 03:48:13.267
overlay since we

03:48:13.767 --> 03:48:17.538
were looking at seeing power generation potentially develop near industrial

03:48:17.570 --> 03:48:21.186
that was already underway with RTI overlay zoning that was already

03:48:21.219 --> 03:48:24.963
in existence. We'd heard at that time

03:48:25.463 --> 03:48:29.116
that there were challenges of taking this approach, and we

03:48:29.616 --> 03:48:32.979
couldn't address that immediately at that point, and so we decided to make some changes.

03:48:33.894 --> 03:48:37.122
We decided as a city at that point to

03:48:37.266 --> 03:48:40.124
carry out several objectives: to learn more about energy,

03:48:40.783 --> 03:48:44.556
to share better information with the public, and to return

03:48:45.056 --> 03:48:47.719
with a new solution in March of 2025.

03:48:48.811 --> 03:48:53.275
After the legislative session in 2025, we received HB 249,

03:48:53.387 --> 03:48:57.150
SB 132, which also changed the landscape of

03:48:57.182 --> 03:49:01.037
power generation development in Utah. Following that,

03:49:01.053 --> 03:49:04.310
we also had our town halls— our town hall.

03:49:05.637 --> 03:49:09.527
The city council visited Idaho National Labs. We had an energy page created

03:49:10.027 --> 03:49:13.722
with a lot of great resources for the public to better understand energy.

03:49:15.442 --> 03:49:19.189
We had planned events and we had done a bunch of other additional learning.

03:49:19.880 --> 03:49:23.746
Staff in particular They met with multiple companies

03:49:23.860 --> 03:49:27.482
and multiple experts to try and understand the technologies and

03:49:27.595 --> 03:49:29.702
the different risks and things like that as much as possible.

03:49:31.389 --> 03:49:35.374
We then created the first iteration of this alternative energy overlay zone.

03:49:35.968 --> 03:49:39.296
We received sign-off from the Planning Commission at that point, but the City Council asked

03:49:39.344 --> 03:49:43.407
for some revisions. The 2026

03:49:43.520 --> 03:49:47.582
legislative session followed, bringing us SB 135 and HB 136. HB 78,

03:49:47.599 --> 03:49:51.734
which also changed some things about the frameworks

03:49:52.234 --> 03:49:55.401
as far as how energy projects are approved in Utah. And then

03:49:55.578 --> 03:49:58.864
finally, we proposed revisions to the city

03:49:58.880 --> 03:50:02.327
council a couple months ago for

03:50:02.359 --> 03:50:05.520
the alternative energy overlay zone in addition to

03:50:05.811 --> 03:50:08.914
other enhancements. And the city council's feedback

03:50:08.978 --> 03:50:12.243
at the time was that it was in a good place. We took that to

03:50:12.743 --> 03:50:16.944
the Planning Commission, who also signed off on the revisions. And that's

03:50:16.993 --> 03:50:20.823
why we're here now. Just a little bit more on the

03:50:20.855 --> 03:50:24.513
education side of things. All of you know, because a lot

03:50:25.013 --> 03:50:29.571
of you were participating in these, it's not just been about nuclear

03:50:30.071 --> 03:50:33.991
as well. We've tried to really understand everything we can about

03:50:34.331 --> 03:50:37.827
power generation in every respect as possible.

03:50:38.058 --> 03:50:41.574
So these council members and mayor, they've visited 3

03:50:42.074 --> 03:50:44.513
different natural gas facilities. We've toured solar facilities,

03:50:45.428 --> 03:50:48.993
so on and so forth. We visited University of Utah

03:50:49.185 --> 03:50:51.256
and had communications with Brigham Young University.

03:50:52.734 --> 03:50:57.037
And there's a lot of other things that haven't been on here previously. Most recently,

03:50:57.294 --> 03:51:01.950
as was mentioned by the mayor, I also traveled to D.C.

03:51:02.047 --> 03:51:05.784
a couple weeks ago. Visited with a number of agencies there,

03:51:05.897 --> 03:51:10.061
but one of those was the Nuclear Regulatory Commission to

03:51:10.998 --> 03:51:14.688
get some additional insights from them as far as what we can be doing

03:51:14.802 --> 03:51:15.224
better here.

03:51:18.537 --> 03:51:21.364
As we get into some of the details of what this overlay zone is,

03:51:21.380 --> 03:51:25.285
I just want to help kind of lay this summary out that the approach

03:51:25.785 --> 03:51:29.392
here was that we wanted to have that careful balance of attracting energy projects

03:51:29.424 --> 03:51:33.012
that provide great economic opportunity, ensuring proper protections

03:51:33.044 --> 03:51:35.828
and oversight is in place to protect residents and their properties.

03:51:37.746 --> 03:51:41.072
As a reminder, we're proposing this alternative energy overlay

03:51:41.572 --> 03:51:45.249
zone with no geography in consideration at this point.

03:51:46.037 --> 03:51:49.557
That inherently gives additional layers of review because

03:51:49.590 --> 03:51:53.233
someone would need to come and ask for this zone to be applied to the

03:51:53.733 --> 03:51:57.723
property. There will be other additional layers of review within our

03:51:57.852 --> 03:52:01.411
community as well, between the development agreements and permits and things like that as well.

03:52:02.180 --> 03:52:05.635
But also having no geography assigned does inherently create an opportunity to put a

03:52:05.701 --> 03:52:07.543
cap on certain energy types as well.

03:52:10.120 --> 03:52:14.024
There are multiple layers of oversight and approval, and I think this is really important

03:52:14.524 --> 03:52:18.509
for the public to know, is that just by the city creating

03:52:18.541 --> 03:52:22.362
this overlay zone does not necessarily mean that a power generation project

03:52:22.426 --> 03:52:25.798
can happen. There are multiple layers at

03:52:26.298 --> 03:52:29.667
the state level and several layers at the federal level that also need to be

03:52:31.080 --> 03:52:34.854
approved and completed before anything could ever

03:52:35.354 --> 03:52:39.012
actually happen here in the city. So as an example of this,

03:52:39.093 --> 03:52:42.288
this has been something we shared in the past. On the federal side, this is

03:52:42.320 --> 03:52:45.708
an example of the multiple agencies that could be involved

03:52:46.208 --> 03:52:49.306
any one project depending on the type of energy used or the location of it.

03:52:50.936 --> 03:52:55.098
And on the state side, there's also an extensive list of different agencies

03:52:55.130 --> 03:52:58.244
that have to give sign-off. So it's not an easy

03:52:58.260 --> 03:53:01.745
process to get a power generation facility constructed by any

03:53:02.245 --> 03:53:05.431
means. Even though we would be coming in with our

03:53:05.512 --> 03:53:09.190
own extensive requirements and regulations and things

03:53:09.690 --> 03:53:13.873
like that, the chances are that ours would

03:53:13.889 --> 03:53:17.460
be, you know, somewhat minor compared to everything

03:53:17.960 --> 03:53:20.843
they would have had to have gone through before then. For us,

03:53:21.134 --> 03:53:25.439
our— we have 3 major things that we're handling through

03:53:25.939 --> 03:53:29.032
the alternative energy overlay zone, the rezone application.

03:53:30.190 --> 03:53:33.986
The rezone application for the alternative energy overlay zone requires submission

03:53:34.244 --> 03:53:38.077
of a whole bunch of materials and still also gives

03:53:38.577 --> 03:53:42.080
us the ability to request additional materials if we don't feel like there

03:53:42.128 --> 03:53:44.404
is sufficient information provided.

03:53:46.240 --> 03:53:49.569
We do require information on the property, the timeline,

03:53:49.618 --> 03:53:52.525
the water use, the types of energy to be used, and so on and so

03:53:53.025 --> 03:53:57.197
forth. There's all sorts of different criteria for evaluation

03:53:57.568 --> 03:54:00.743
and things like that. And then additionally, the building permit also requires

03:54:00.791 --> 03:54:04.112
extensive information, some of these things which would be

03:54:04.726 --> 03:54:08.122
already created as part of the federal review process, but we want to be able

03:54:08.622 --> 03:54:12.024
to monitor those and be able to view those and study them ourselves as

03:54:12.092 --> 03:54:15.482
well. And we also require

03:54:15.853 --> 03:54:18.918
copies of all the federal and state permits so that we understand that we're not

03:54:19.418 --> 03:54:22.775
getting ahead of those projects or those requirements.

03:54:24.726 --> 03:54:28.488
The development agreement requirement also is a little

03:54:28.988 --> 03:54:32.271
more open-ended, and intentionally so. There's no obligations

03:54:32.771 --> 03:54:35.330
that we have to complete that within a certain amount of time. Gives us a

03:54:35.346 --> 03:54:39.299
lot more flexibility so that if there are specific projects

03:54:40.757 --> 03:54:44.531
that are unique in some way or, you know,

03:54:44.548 --> 03:54:47.976
have special considerations that we want to capture, the development agreement

03:54:48.476 --> 03:54:52.298
will be the ideal opportunity to take care of those. And we

03:54:52.798 --> 03:54:56.635
can really use that to tool in a very flexible way to accomplish a lot

03:54:57.135 --> 03:55:00.791
of things. Overall,

03:55:00.823 --> 03:55:04.697
the alternative energy overlay zone creates standards for our

03:55:04.762 --> 03:55:07.809
energy— our power generation projects in

03:55:08.166 --> 03:55:11.409
this city. What you won't find

03:55:12.039 --> 03:55:15.896
is consideration of wind, hydro, or coal in

03:55:15.928 --> 03:55:19.557
the alternative energy overlay zone, and that's because all 3 of those aren't really

03:55:20.057 --> 03:55:21.618
compatible with our area.

03:55:23.720 --> 03:55:27.673
You will see common among all the energy types and considerations

03:55:28.173 --> 03:55:30.630
for lighting, fire safety, seismic safety, dust control,

03:55:30.839 --> 03:55:33.750
traffic controls, hazard materials handling,

03:55:33.814 --> 03:55:37.149
screening, and buffering. You'll see some specific standards by

03:55:37.165 --> 03:55:40.693
energy types, and just some examples or specifics on batteries

03:55:40.726 --> 03:55:44.318
with fire safety and financial assurances for

03:55:44.818 --> 03:55:48.241
solar and things like that. You'll see some absence of some

03:55:48.274 --> 03:55:51.824
things with geotechnical and hazardous materials only

03:55:52.324 --> 03:55:55.374
because some of these things are already reviewed at

03:55:55.874 --> 03:55:58.207
the federal and state levels, and so it would be redundant for us to do

03:55:58.707 --> 03:56:02.114
so. Although, as I mentioned before, we would want to see their reviews of that—

03:56:02.371 --> 03:56:04.188
of those considerations.

03:56:08.210 --> 03:56:11.862
I just wanted to make a note as well, as I mentioned briefly,

03:56:12.058 --> 03:56:14.933
with the federal side of things,

03:56:15.143 --> 03:56:18.455
specifically talking about nuclear with the Nuclear Regulatory Commission.

03:56:18.535 --> 03:56:22.057
So as I mentioned, I met with the Nuclear Regulatory Commission a couple weeks

03:56:22.181 --> 03:56:25.795
ago. As far as I'm aware, and I think as

03:56:25.828 --> 03:56:29.702
far as they're aware also, this would be the first time that the Nuclear

03:56:30.202 --> 03:56:33.841
Regulatory Commission has ever reviewed a local zoning ordinance

03:56:34.215 --> 03:56:37.926
for siting of a nuclear facility. I couldn't

03:56:38.426 --> 03:56:41.388
consider it an official review because they've just never had that sort of a process

03:56:41.888 --> 03:56:45.386
before. But they did review our overlay zone

03:56:45.482 --> 03:56:49.882
and only found one incompatibility with their regulations,

03:56:50.139 --> 03:56:53.511
and that being is our definition of a microreactor.

03:56:54.153 --> 03:56:57.702
It should be pointed out the NRC doesn't actually have a definition right

03:56:58.202 --> 03:57:01.122
now of a microreactor, so that's part of the problem is that we're also in

03:57:01.234 --> 03:57:04.847
some ways paving the way for that, is that we're creating definitions that the federal

03:57:05.347 --> 03:57:08.684
government does not yet have either. So we may have to alter that in the

03:57:09.184 --> 03:57:13.694
future. But otherwise, they had found that everything made

03:57:14.194 --> 03:57:18.607
sense and that everything that we had in there was not stepping

03:57:18.639 --> 03:57:21.208
on toes, so to speak, as far as what they do, and that we're in

03:57:21.708 --> 03:57:25.608
our proper lane for everything. There were also previous considerations

03:57:25.640 --> 03:57:28.819
and questions about the Atomic Energy Act of 1954.

03:57:30.294 --> 03:57:34.460
about whether or not that particular act gives the

03:57:34.960 --> 03:57:39.075
federal government ability to basically overrule anything

03:57:39.107 --> 03:57:43.226
that a local government does in terms of approval

03:57:43.754 --> 03:57:45.998
or denial of a nuclear project.

03:57:47.055 --> 03:57:50.917
So I directly asked them about that, and their

03:57:52.007 --> 03:57:55.728
message back to us was that essentially That's

03:57:56.228 --> 03:58:00.039
not how it's meant to be interpreted. It's meant to be interpreted that local governments

03:58:00.167 --> 03:58:03.835
are not to interfere, essentially,

03:58:04.012 --> 03:58:06.040
with the licensing process of the NRC.

03:58:08.053 --> 03:58:12.046
We cannot impose controls on them, but if we have our own

03:58:12.062 --> 03:58:14.995
local ordinances, we have our own zoning requirements and things like that,

03:58:15.060 --> 03:58:18.210
we have every right to do those things. So the NRC

03:58:18.710 --> 03:58:22.303
will do what the NRC does. But we still have every right to deny

03:58:23.220 --> 03:58:26.098
an energy project according to our own ordinances.

03:58:26.613 --> 03:58:29.765
The federal government does not step in in that way.

03:58:37.550 --> 03:58:40.951
Yeah, last little thing too is the NRC wanted to make it very clear too

03:58:41.146 --> 03:58:44.775
that when going through the licensing process,

03:58:46.748 --> 03:58:50.763
Public support of a project is a major consideration

03:58:50.779 --> 03:58:54.098
factor. They do their own analysis on that

03:58:54.598 --> 03:58:57.710
to understand that there is local support for nuclear.

03:58:58.163 --> 03:59:01.418
They don't want to approve a license for a nuclear

03:59:01.467 --> 03:59:04.916
facility if it's broadly unsupported.

03:59:08.925 --> 03:59:12.872
So we do have a ton of regulations and a ton of controls within

03:59:12.904 --> 03:59:16.336
the AEOZ, as we call it for short. And so I think

03:59:16.658 --> 03:59:19.393
one of the natural questions you might ask is why would anyone even want to

03:59:19.893 --> 03:59:23.688
use the AEOZ if it's going to have a whole bunch of requirements around

03:59:23.737 --> 03:59:26.991
it? The really what we're trying to accomplish out of this is that it

03:59:27.491 --> 03:59:32.381
creates an actual pathway for success for a project to be able to to

03:59:32.397 --> 03:59:36.221
happen within the state of Utah and specifically within Eagle Mountain City.

03:59:36.770 --> 03:59:40.062
It it creates that clear pathway for approved uses,

03:59:40.720 --> 03:59:44.317
and it gives a reason— a company reasonable understanding of local support.

03:59:45.794 --> 03:59:49.808
The only other real consideration here is that it cuts down the site plan process

03:59:50.308 --> 03:59:53.598
specifically to be reviewed by the DRC. Everything else still

03:59:54.098 --> 03:59:57.483
gets reviewed by the City Council and Planning Commission as, as it normally would.

03:59:58.238 --> 04:00:02.252
It's the only thing that is reduced in process-wise

04:00:02.752 --> 04:00:06.620
is the site plan. With the risks of just not having this

04:00:06.668 --> 04:00:09.767
tool, we have mentioned, of course, we need the

04:00:09.783 --> 04:00:13.412
economic development. We need energy for economic development. And so

04:00:13.476 --> 04:00:16.382
without promoting energy development,

04:00:16.462 --> 04:00:20.460
we potentially risk having energy or economic development

04:00:20.798 --> 04:00:24.202
continue to maintain its current pace, which has not been adequate

04:00:24.330 --> 04:00:27.702
at this point. Development agreements would remain the only

04:00:28.202 --> 04:00:31.335
tool which we have to try and regulate any energy projects that come through.

04:00:33.232 --> 04:00:36.798
And then power generation could be sited outside of Eagle Mountain.

04:00:37.896 --> 04:00:40.933
If far enough away, our area potentially loses out on that

04:00:40.981 --> 04:00:44.050
power. We don't have any guarantees that that power comes to our area.

04:00:44.928 --> 04:00:48.117
If it's right outside our border, we have no say in the approval standards

04:00:48.166 --> 04:00:49.979
or location of where that's done.

04:00:52.271 --> 04:00:56.254
And then we have the inability to attract projects Which

04:00:56.287 --> 04:00:59.584
means we still have restrictions on current projects, current data centers,

04:00:59.616 --> 04:01:03.125
current other projects we're trying to attract and things like that.

04:01:07.267 --> 04:01:11.139
We did make some updates, as mentioned, to the AEOZ recently that

04:01:11.639 --> 04:01:14.369
was brought to council over the past— a couple months ago.

04:01:14.771 --> 04:01:17.949
So just wanted to make sure that we run through these really fast again.

04:01:18.653 --> 04:01:21.813
We expanded the definitions for abandonment. Had new

04:01:22.313 --> 04:01:26.387
definitions for abandonment and ancillary building of microreactor, removed the

04:01:26.887 --> 04:01:31.090
definition for cooling tower, and separated out some of these definitions

04:01:31.122 --> 04:01:33.417
for small modular reactor units and facilities.

04:01:34.573 --> 04:01:38.473
We have made a couple adjustments to the approval processes,

04:01:38.907 --> 04:01:43.369
specifically in nuclear, so that we're not doing

04:01:43.465 --> 04:01:45.921
things in the wrong order when it comes to state and federal and local.

04:01:47.157 --> 04:01:49.741
And then had some exemptions with microreactors. Thank you.

04:01:50.303 --> 04:01:53.595
Tried to close some loopholes, and then added administrative

04:01:54.095 --> 04:01:57.769
appeal process. We put restrictions on technologies, wanted to specifically

04:01:58.269 --> 04:02:01.607
make sure that we— it was understood that natural gas, it needed to be low

04:02:01.623 --> 04:02:05.846
water use, preferably air-cooled and fuel cell-generated systems.

04:02:06.600 --> 04:02:10.358
We didn't eliminate combined cycles specifically, even though

04:02:10.858 --> 04:02:13.842
that is typically the higher water use, only because there are hybrid versions now that

04:02:14.342 --> 04:02:17.729
exist that could be used. But the emphasis has been

04:02:17.761 --> 04:02:22.017
put on closed-loop water systems. And then with nuclear, we specified

04:02:22.517 --> 04:02:26.258
more directly which types of nuclear we wanted

04:02:26.758 --> 04:02:31.253
to see, eliminating some of these higher water uses and the more— the

04:02:31.753 --> 04:02:35.879
older generations, the 2nd and 3rd generation nuclear power technologies

04:02:35.896 --> 04:02:39.381
like the pressurized water reactors, boiling water reactors, light water reactors, so on and so

04:02:39.445 --> 04:02:42.735
forth. And so we're only promoting the 4th gen walkaway safe,

04:02:42.799 --> 04:02:46.358
low to no water use designs, where the physics of these

04:02:46.727 --> 04:02:51.520
are literally different from older generations, where as

04:02:51.616 --> 04:02:55.576
I've visited with companies and visited with different laboratories and things like

04:02:56.076 --> 04:02:59.471
that, they've, and even the NRC has also asserted this, that a lot

04:02:59.971 --> 04:03:03.030
of these new technologies are designed in a way where it's,

04:03:04.296 --> 04:03:08.601
there's really no way that you can cause an

04:03:09.101 --> 04:03:12.407
overheating or a disaster of the kinds that we've heard in the

04:03:12.907 --> 04:03:16.422
past. We've added language to say

04:03:16.922 --> 04:03:19.576
that we're not obligated to give water for these uses, that water will need to

04:03:20.076 --> 04:03:23.737
be purchased and we don't need to be the seller. The projects need to demonstrate

04:03:24.237 --> 04:03:28.150
water is procured before any permit is given so that they're not coming back and,

04:03:28.651 --> 04:03:31.949
you know, saying we're stuck, please save us. You know, they need to be

04:03:32.449 --> 04:03:33.428
able to demonstrate that. water is there.

04:03:35.627 --> 04:03:38.815
And they need to make sure that they're providing better information about what water

04:03:39.315 --> 04:03:41.564
is needed for the different phases of development.

04:03:42.800 --> 04:03:46.841
We added caps to the types of energy for natural gas and

04:03:46.857 --> 04:03:50.624
nuclear. And so nuclear, this zone would only ever approve

04:03:50.656 --> 04:03:54.083
up to 3 gigawatts of generation, and then natural gas up to 2 gigawatts

04:03:54.583 --> 04:03:58.381
of generation. And then other regulatory factors, as just an FYI,

04:03:58.429 --> 04:03:59.969
mainland limit this further anyway.

04:04:01.692 --> 04:04:04.641
And then this also can be utilized to limit the total power generation projects of

04:04:04.657 --> 04:04:08.348
the city to what we need and not to be a source of power generation

04:04:08.461 --> 04:04:12.643
for the state as a whole. We added some language about decommissioning,

04:04:13.548 --> 04:04:17.381
just trying to enhance decommissioning planning language, the reviews

04:04:17.881 --> 04:04:21.481
of solar and things like that. There wasn't any changes to nuclear

04:04:21.755 --> 04:04:26.015
only because the NRC already has very specific

04:04:26.515 --> 04:04:28.313
requirements for decommissioning, and so for us to add more on there would be redundant.

04:04:29.420 --> 04:04:32.805
And natural gas also, we didn't do much on there as well, only because the

04:04:32.869 --> 04:04:36.222
cost to decommission is extremely low and in some

04:04:36.238 --> 04:04:39.431
cases near zero, just because of the

04:04:39.447 --> 04:04:43.137
high scrap value, the low impact on the terrain, and things like

04:04:43.637 --> 04:04:47.051
that. We added a lot more things related to safety and emergency

04:04:47.068 --> 04:04:50.613
planning as well, a lot more information on battery storage, just make

04:04:51.113 --> 04:04:54.701
sure everything is complying with international fire codes and so on and so

04:04:55.201 --> 04:04:58.740
forth. Geothermal, trying to make sure we're covering everything there.

04:04:59.430 --> 04:05:02.556
And then solar as well. There's a number of things that just we

04:05:03.056 --> 04:05:07.509
hadn't even considered before that we learned about later, about glare hazards and

04:05:07.605 --> 04:05:10.459
things like that to roads and aircraft and things like that.

04:05:10.764 --> 04:05:14.146
So last thing with architectural standards,

04:05:14.884 --> 04:05:18.238
we just added a couple of exemptions under this only because originally

04:05:18.495 --> 04:05:21.481
we were trying to treat this similar to the RTI overlay zone where we want

04:05:21.981 --> 04:05:26.025
to have some consistency in the architectural standards and some high-quality architectural standards.

04:05:26.700 --> 04:05:29.959
But there are some structures that would likely

04:05:29.975 --> 04:05:33.716
be constructed as part of these facilities that just can't abide by certain

04:05:33.989 --> 04:05:38.919
elevated architectural standards. They have to meet certain federal

04:05:39.419 --> 04:05:42.082
standards and they just unfortunately don't always look pretty.

04:05:42.982 --> 04:05:46.581
They just have to meet those safety standards. So we just make some exceptions

04:05:46.693 --> 04:05:49.666
where there has to be a certain design of a certain facility in a certain

04:05:49.730 --> 04:05:51.955
way to meet federal standards.

04:05:54.120 --> 04:05:57.552
In summary, the alternative energy overlay zone

04:05:58.052 --> 04:06:01.403
balances the ability to attract power generation facilities that

04:06:01.903 --> 04:06:05.533
will greatly enhance our economic development outcomes while also ensuring our home and

04:06:06.033 --> 04:06:09.815
residents are properly safeguarded and leadership is able to effectively evaluate

04:06:10.315 --> 04:06:14.616
projects that arise. I'm happy to answer any questions at

04:06:15.116 --> 04:06:18.547
this time. Can you

04:06:19.389 --> 04:06:22.691
clarify why the difference between 25 megawatts and more and

04:06:22.902 --> 04:06:26.785
25 megawatts and less? Yeah, so the,

04:06:27.334 --> 04:06:31.291
the 25 megawatts is kind of the threshold at which we set our definition

04:06:31.355 --> 04:06:35.512
for microreactors. versus typical smaller modular

04:06:36.012 --> 04:06:40.143
reactors. And that was just basically based on— we had met

04:06:40.643 --> 04:06:42.772
with multiple microreactor developers,

04:06:44.157 --> 04:06:48.199
and none of them exceeded 25 megawatts as a single

04:06:48.699 --> 04:06:52.517
reactor development. And so without a full definition from

04:06:52.872 --> 04:06:56.774
the NRC, that seemed to be a safe limit to where

04:06:56.822 --> 04:06:59.856
we crossed that line. Okay. And so basically the approval

04:06:59.872 --> 04:07:03.179
process is going to be a little more streamlined for something that's less than 25

04:07:03.679 --> 04:07:07.097
megawatts? Potentially. It depends on how they put— how they

04:07:07.178 --> 04:07:10.967
use that. Because microreactors, you do a single microreactor,

04:07:12.155 --> 04:07:15.142
then yes, it could be a much more streamlined process. It could be like a

04:07:15.543 --> 04:07:19.365
2-megawatt-something sort of facility, and it has an extremely

04:07:19.381 --> 04:07:22.945
low impact. These are also designed to be extremely safe and things like that.

04:07:22.993 --> 04:07:24.839
They're treated differently. by the NRC anyway.

04:07:26.577 --> 04:07:29.848
But much like modular reactors, microreactors can

04:07:29.897 --> 04:07:33.509
also be strung together. And so if you string together,

04:07:33.946 --> 04:07:38.072
you know, 10, 12, 50, whatever, then you

04:07:38.572 --> 04:07:42.085
start getting into a nuclear facility, is how we're trying to define

04:07:42.117 --> 04:07:45.524
that at that point. And so when you start stringing some

04:07:46.024 --> 04:07:49.294
together and you get over that 25 megawatts, then that process goes back to just

04:07:49.326 --> 04:07:52.939
the regular process. And we've tried to look at different ways at which that could

04:07:52.955 --> 04:07:56.311
be potentially abused, or there's loopholes or whatever, where it's like,

04:07:56.937 --> 04:08:00.630
you know, you might try and have each microreactor on a separate

04:08:00.726 --> 04:08:04.371
parcel or something like that, or they're trying to, you know,

04:08:04.419 --> 04:08:07.888
use them for different purposes, or they're trying to do— there's all sorts of different

04:08:08.388 --> 04:08:11.677
ways that you could potentially try and take advantage of loopholes.

04:08:11.741 --> 04:08:15.033
And so we've tried to close every single possible loophole on there so that At

04:08:15.533 --> 04:08:19.513
any point at which you get over that 25 megawatts, we're treating that

04:08:20.013 --> 04:08:23.430
as a regular nuclear reactor at that point. All right, and then if you could

04:08:23.607 --> 04:08:27.557
remind me, so we require a bond for decommissioning for the BES

04:08:28.057 --> 04:08:32.422
and the solar. We don't require a bond for any of the other energy

04:08:32.454 --> 04:08:35.826
types? For geothermal, we would. There was a bond, there was a

04:08:36.326 --> 04:08:39.583
bond in there. Maybe I missed that. Okay, but the others we don't, and is

04:08:40.083 --> 04:08:43.469
that because Why would we not require

04:08:43.533 --> 04:08:47.210
a bond for those? Like, say, let's say they go bankrupt and

04:08:47.226 --> 04:08:50.357
suddenly we're left with this facility. Yeah.

04:08:50.919 --> 04:08:53.986
So a bond would typically help us in that regard. So some

04:08:54.486 --> 04:08:57.037
of these product types, these energy types, wouldn't have a bond.

04:08:57.406 --> 04:09:01.356
Should we require the bond, and why do we not? So with

04:09:01.597 --> 04:09:05.531
nuclear and natural gas in particular, nuclear already has their own requirements

04:09:06.031 --> 04:09:09.296
for the those sorts of things when it comes to decommissioning, be able to have

04:09:10.218 --> 04:09:14.018
the funding resources to go ahead and do the decommissioning

04:09:14.518 --> 04:09:16.296
process and whatnot as part of their licensing process.

04:09:17.893 --> 04:09:21.831
And then the natural gas side of things, the cost

04:09:22.331 --> 04:09:25.639
to decommission and to, you know,

04:09:26.123 --> 04:09:28.320
remove those facilities is extremely low.

04:09:29.877 --> 04:09:33.177
We could add in a bonding process. If we wanted to, if you want to

04:09:33.242 --> 04:09:37.866
make sure that that is you know safeguarded. But from

04:09:38.011 --> 04:09:41.310
the experts that I've talked to, there's a lot of value in a lot of

04:09:41.810 --> 04:09:45.222
the equipment, even if it's abandoned. That even just the scrap

04:09:45.722 --> 04:09:49.130
value of the metal alone would generally cover the costs of removal.

04:09:51.066 --> 04:09:55.093
Can I ask Council Member Whiting just a quick kind of follow-up discussion

04:09:55.593 --> 04:09:59.364
with that? When we talk about A decommissioning

04:09:59.461 --> 04:10:03.046
bond. I know that

04:10:03.110 --> 04:10:06.631
some are governed by federal requirements and they have

04:10:06.647 --> 04:10:10.365
to bond and do those things because of federal requirements. What would you think about

04:10:10.865 --> 04:10:14.069
having something that was like requiring a decommissioning plan?

04:10:14.748 --> 04:10:18.662
So whether it's there's a bond that's required or

04:10:19.034 --> 04:10:22.461
this is what would happen in the event of a decommissioning situation.

04:10:22.509 --> 04:10:26.527
Yeah. Because I understand that we don't have to require a bond because

04:10:27.027 --> 04:10:30.710
it's already required by another bigger entity than us, but maybe

04:10:30.935 --> 04:10:34.249
having that be a part of the approval process would help

04:10:34.510 --> 04:10:37.807
kind of address some of the concerns that I think maybe you're getting at.

04:10:38.490 --> 04:10:42.144
Yeah, and I think most of them did require a decommissioning plan, like all of

04:10:42.644 --> 04:10:45.798
them might have, I don't remember. And I like the idea that the

04:10:45.944 --> 04:10:50.058
plan's included. Okay. Yeah. Evan, I— sorry,

04:10:50.138 --> 04:10:52.670
I just— it's on the same topic. I, I just wanted to ask, uh,

04:10:54.064 --> 04:10:57.301
a lot of these plants, like I'm thinking IPP for instance, they've been down there

04:10:57.365 --> 04:10:59.641
for 100 years and maybe 100 more.

04:11:00.971 --> 04:11:03.711
If— yeah, I know, I work down there too.

04:11:04.336 --> 04:11:07.814
The, uh, at the time that they build it,

04:11:07.846 --> 04:11:11.515
they place a bond at today's value, and 100 years from now that

04:11:11.547 --> 04:11:16.197
value goes up. What makes that How does the bond

04:11:16.697 --> 04:11:20.290
increase with like inflation or costs? Is there a mechanism to— So

04:11:20.675 --> 04:11:24.270
I asked that question when we were looking at solar before with a development agreement,

04:11:24.286 --> 04:11:27.223
in particular landowner that was doing something on the south.

04:11:27.736 --> 04:11:30.946
And she had said something about like every 2 to 3

04:11:31.446 --> 04:11:35.263
years or every year, they reevaluate the amount that's bonded

04:11:35.279 --> 04:11:38.408
for so that it has to be enough to cover the cost.

04:11:38.762 --> 04:11:40.607
Is that your understanding too, Evan, or?

04:11:44.639 --> 04:11:48.128
I haven't gotten into the specifics on that, so I'm afraid I don't know

04:11:48.193 --> 04:11:51.367
the answer to that question. That's why

04:11:51.867 --> 04:11:54.537
I brought it up. I think it's important that maybe that's included, that we evaluate

04:11:55.229 --> 04:11:58.432
5 years, 10 years. You know, we know what the last 5 years have done,

04:11:58.786 --> 04:12:02.327
and specifically the last 10 years, that those

04:12:02.360 --> 04:12:06.094
bonds are renewed or updated or evaluated at least.

04:12:06.560 --> 04:12:10.234
I'm just throwing that out for— any

04:12:10.734 --> 04:12:13.035
other questions? If— go ahead. I have a ton.

04:12:14.262 --> 04:12:17.298
One of the things that I kind of gleaned from this, and thank you,

04:12:17.314 --> 04:12:19.612
by the way, for the presentation and all the work that you've put into this.

04:12:20.046 --> 04:12:22.874
This has been like a 2-year process and we've all done a ton of homework.

04:12:23.195 --> 04:12:25.288
So thank you for putting it all together in one presentation.

04:12:27.139 --> 04:12:30.478
You had talked about how there are certain standards and

04:12:31.872 --> 04:12:35.119
I kind of wrote a note saying standards, if we rely on federal,

04:12:35.810 --> 04:12:38.994
what happens if the federal government changes

04:12:39.508 --> 04:12:41.123
their requirements or standards?

04:12:44.259 --> 04:12:48.926
What happens with that? I mean— like

04:12:48.942 --> 04:12:51.920
if they reduce them, I guess is probably the best question.

04:12:53.416 --> 04:12:56.946
If I can use, I guess, nuclear as an example, because that's about

04:12:57.446 --> 04:13:00.062
my best— I know best, because that's the questions we've gotten the most on.

04:13:01.490 --> 04:13:05.368
Because I specifically asked the Nuclear Regulatory Commission about that.

04:13:05.984 --> 04:13:10.008
I think it's pretty well known that they're going through reviews of

04:13:10.090 --> 04:13:14.027
their standards, and a lot of people I think are perceiving

04:13:14.059 --> 04:13:16.598
that as a reduction in standards.

04:13:18.237 --> 04:13:22.713
The way it was described to me, and this comes from— I literally

04:13:23.213 --> 04:13:27.198
met with almost the entirety of the Office of Advanced Reactors at the NRC.

04:13:28.947 --> 04:13:31.442
Nobody is reducing any standards.

04:13:32.920 --> 04:13:36.594
