06:16:14.000 --> 06:16:17.527
I provided a document showing the 30 locations— 31,

06:16:17.640 --> 06:16:21.104
including this one that Rich is also proposing. And our goal

06:16:21.136 --> 06:16:24.360
here is to collect data that we can evaluate,

06:16:24.505 --> 06:16:27.955
review, and understand what the speeding is, the severity

06:16:27.971 --> 06:16:32.262
of it, road conditions, and to identify

06:16:32.762 --> 06:16:36.375
potential solutions for the area. With this

06:16:36.875 --> 06:16:40.281
information, we can then come back to council and provide recommendations to

06:16:40.618 --> 06:16:43.894
help mitigate speedings in these locations throughout the city.

06:16:44.969 --> 06:16:50.686
We're looking to estimated budget— this would be not to exceed $50,000.

06:16:50.750 --> 06:16:54.186
We would then hire a consulting engineer who would collect

06:16:54.507 --> 06:16:57.702
vehicle data speeds based on the 85th

06:16:57.718 --> 06:17:00.191
percentile, vehicle classifications,

06:17:01.347 --> 06:17:05.056
peak traffic conditions, road geometry, site conditions, and then crash history.

06:17:06.229 --> 06:17:10.437
The consultant would then review the data and then start to evaluate

06:17:10.742 --> 06:17:14.324
recommendations for those areas, provide engineering construction

06:17:14.678 --> 06:17:18.260
estimates for those improvements if required, and then prioritize

06:17:18.356 --> 06:17:21.810
the ranking of implementation. Some of these

06:17:22.310 --> 06:17:24.910
recommendations could include, but not limited, are no action,

06:17:25.681 --> 06:17:28.861
right, radar detection feedback signs, speed limit modifications,

06:17:29.455 --> 06:17:32.632
pavement markings, bulb-outs, curb extensions,

06:17:33.241 --> 06:17:35.427
elevated crosswalks, or other solutions.

06:17:37.250 --> 06:17:40.254
And the benefits of this is that we would kind of begin— this would not

06:17:40.754 --> 06:17:43.950
be just a one-and-done. We would look at this as a needed basis. This could

06:17:44.450 --> 06:17:48.274
be annual, you know, as needed. But it would provide a system

06:17:48.774 --> 06:17:52.522
where we would collect data before making decisions. It would help us prioritize

06:17:52.699 --> 06:17:55.563
areas in the city that need improvements and others that do not.

06:17:56.818 --> 06:18:00.683
And it would also help avoid any unintended traffic or any

06:18:01.039 --> 06:18:04.403
operational impacts. We already know a lot of roads are pretty congested,

06:18:04.435 --> 06:18:07.146
and the last thing we want to do is cause more delays on our roadways.

06:18:08.224 --> 06:18:11.700
And this would provide a consistent process for future speeding requests

06:18:12.200 --> 06:18:15.643
in the future. Speeding is a tricky animal. It's not

06:18:16.143 --> 06:18:19.640
something that's, you know, just indicative of the road type and conditions.

06:18:19.817 --> 06:18:23.655
Every situation is unique, and there's no magic silver bullet. And so

06:18:24.155 --> 06:18:27.272
our goal here is to identify the right improvement at the right location and

06:18:27.289 --> 06:18:29.722
not apply one solution everywhere.

